"""Authored topic depth and three distinct worked examples for programming and algorithms."""
from . import P, E

P('3-c-memory-c-control-flow', 'Programming in C', '''To trace a C program, separate the values of objects from the path of execution. Give every declaration its own object, including a parameter that copies a caller's value. Record a loop's condition before entering its body and its update after completing or continuing the body. Function calls can modify an object through a copied pointer even though ordinary scalar parameters remain independent. Check the control statement's actual rule: switch selects an entry label but falls through subsequent statements until a break or the end. Assume initialized values, valid addresses and arithmetic representable in the stated types before assigning a deterministic result.''',
E('fundamental', 'Trace int x=3,s=0; while(x>0){s+=x; --x;} What are final x and s?', r'First iteration::s=3,\ x=2::The initial true condition permits addition before decrement.~Remaining iterations::s=3+2+1=6::The next bodies use x=2 and x=1, then leave x=0.~Stop::0>0\ \text{is false}::The condition is checked again without executing another body.', 'x=0 and s=6.', 'The accumulator equals the independent sum 1+2+3; there are three decrements.'),
E('application', 'void change(int v,int *p){v=9; *p=7;} Called as change(x,&a[0]) with x=2 and a=[4,5], what remains?', "Bind arguments::::v copies x=2; p copies the address of a[0].~Execute assignments::::v=9 changes the local parameter. Writing *p=7 changes the caller's array element.~Return::::The local v disappears while x and a remain live in the caller.", 'x=2; a=[7,5].', 'Only a[0] was reachable through p; neither x nor a[1] was assigned by the function.'),
E('trap', 'Trace int y=0; switch(2){case 1:y+=1; case 2:y+=2; case 3:y+=3; break; default:y=9;}', r'Select entry::::The controlling value is two, so execution starts at case 2.~Follow fallthrough::y=0+2+3=5::Without a break after case 2, case 3 also executes.~Exit at break::::The break leaves the switch before default, whose statement is never reached.', 'y=5, rather than 2 or 9.', 'The executed additions are exactly +2 and +3; case labels themselves do not end a branch.'))

P('3-c-memory-pointers', 'Pointers', '''A pointer expression must be interpreted relative to the object and pointed-to type, not merely as an integer address. Keep an alias diagram connecting each pointer variable to its current target. A write through an alias updates that shared object, whereas assigning a new address to the pointer changes only the alias. Pointer differences within one array count elements and already account for element width. The permitted range includes a one-past value for comparison or subtraction, but excludes dereferencing it. A pointer's numeric-looking value cannot establish lifetime or bounds; determine both from the originating object and its allocation before following it.''',
E('fundamental', 'For int a[]={10,20,30}, int *p=a,*q=p; execute *q=11; p++; *p=22. Find a and both targets.', r'Create aliases::::Initially p and q both designate a[0]. Writing through q changes a[0] to 11.~Move only p::::p++ advances p to a[1]; q still designates a[0].~Write the new target::::*p=22 changes a[1], leaving a[2] unchanged.', 'a=[11,22,30]; p targets a[1], q targets a[0].', 'Reading *q gives 11 and p−q gives one element, consistent with distinct current targets.'),
E('application', 'A valid int a[6] has base byte address 1000 and sizeof(int)=4. p=&a[4], q=&a[1]. Find p−q and their byte separation.', r'Locate each address::p=1016,\ q=1004::Scale the two indices by four bytes from the same base.~Subtract typed pointers::p-q=4-1=3::C pointer subtraction reports element count within this array.~Separate byte distance::1016-1004=12::Byte separation is the element count multiplied by element width.', 'p−q=3 elements; byte separation is 12.', 'q+3 equals p; multiplying the typed difference by sizeof(int) reproduces the address difference.'),
E('trap', 'For int a[3], p=a+3. Classify forming p, comparing p==a+3, and reading *p.', r'Form the boundary::::a+3 is the permitted one-past pointer for this array.~Compare the boundary::::Equality comparison with the same one-past expression is valid and true.~Check dereference::::There is no a[3] element, so *p cannot be read as an array value.', 'Formation and comparison are valid; dereferencing p has undefined behavior.', 'The valid element indices are 0,1,2. Permission to represent the end does not create an additional element.'))

P('3-c-memory-pointers', 'Arrays', '''An array type fixes both its element type and extent, and a multidimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays. C lays out each complete inner row contiguously, followed by the next row. Derive offsets from the declared inner extent instead of assuming that two indices contribute equally. Most expression contexts convert an array to its first-element pointer, but sizeof applied directly to an array object uses its complete storage. Array-form parameters are adjusted to pointers, so the same-looking spelling inside a function does not retain the caller's extent. Bounds belong to each subarray as well as to the enclosing object.''',
E('fundamental', 'For int a[2][3] at byte address 200 with four-byte ints, locate a[1][2].', r'Count complete rows::1\cdot3=3::The first index skips one entire row of three ints.~Add the column::3+2=5::Column two is two additional elements within the second row.~Scale to bytes::200+5\cdot4=220::Multiply the total element offset by the int width.', 'a[1][2] begins at byte address 220.', 'The six element addresses are 200,204,208,212,216,220, so the target is the final element.'),
E('application', 'On a machine with four-byte ints and eight-byte pointers, compare sizeof a for int a[7] with sizeof b inside void f(int b[7]).', "Use the actual object::sizeof(a)=7\\cdot4=28::a in the caller is an array object containing seven ints.~Adjust the parameter::::The array-form parameter b has type pointer to int in the function.~Measure that parameter::sizeof(b)=8::sizeof sees a pointer object, not the caller's full array.", 'The sizes are 28 bytes and 8 bytes.', 'Changing the caller array extent changes its object size but does not change the adjusted parameter pointer width.'),
E('trap', 'Given int a[2][3], is a[0][3] a valid way to read a[1][0] because rows are adjacent?', "Identify the inner object::::a[0] is an array containing exactly three ints.~Check its index::::Index three is one past that row and cannot be dereferenced.~Separate layout from permission::::Contiguous neighboring storage does not extend the first row's indexing bounds.", 'No: a[0][3] is out of bounds; use a[1][0].', 'The legitimate column indices in each row are 0 through 2; both indices of a[1][0] obey their extents.'))

P('3-c-memory-storage', 'Storage', '''Use two parallel timelines when reasoning about storage: one for name visibility and another for object existence. Entering a nested block can hide a name without destroying the outer object. Each ordinary function activation creates its own initialized automatic locals, while a static local belongs to one persistent object shared by all activations of that function. Dynamic storage follows explicit allocation and release rather than lexical block entry and exit. Initialization and lifetime must be checked independently; a surviving pointer can outlive its target. Never derive an output by reading a dead object simply because its previous bytes might still occupy the same address.''',
E('fundamental', 'A global int x=5 is shadowed by int x=2 inside a block; the block increments its x once. What values exist inside and after the block?', r'Create separate objects::::The block declaration creates a new automatic x while the global x remains five.~Execute the local increment::2+1=3::The visible name binds to the local object, so the global is untouched.~Leave the scope::::The local lifetime ends and the outer binding becomes visible again.', 'Inside the block local x=3; afterward global x=5.', 'Only one assignment targeted the local object; no statement named the hidden global during the block.'),
E('application', 'In f(n), execute int local=n; static int s=0; s+=n; if(n>0) f(n−1); then print(local,s). Trace f(2), with s initially zero.', r'Descend with separate locals::::Frames retain local values 2,1,0 while their additions make shared s=3.~Print the deepest frame::::f(0) prints (0,3); its local disappears on return.~Resume callers::::f(1) prints (1,3), then f(2) prints (2,3), because s belongs to one persistent object.', 'Printed pairs are (0,3),(1,3),(2,3).', 'The shared sum is independently 2+1+0=3, while each retained local still equals its own argument.'),
E('trap', 'int *make(void){int a=6; return &a;} Can the caller safely read *make()?', "Locate the target::::a is an automatic local object belonging to make's activation.~End its lifetime::::Returning ends that activation and a's lifetime; returning its address does not extend it.~Classify the read::::The caller has no live pointed-to object from which to obtain six.", 'No; dereferencing that returned address is invalid and has no defined result.', 'A static local or suitably managed dynamically allocated object would have a different lifetime; this automatic a does not.'))

P('3-c-memory-c-expressions', 'Expressions', '''Analyze an expression in three passes: determine its grouping, determine the operand types at each operator, then check evaluation and sequencing rules. Parentheses can clarify grouping without imposing an evaluation order between independent operands. Logical operators produce an int truth value of zero or one, whereas bitwise operators combine the individual bits of integer operands. Short-circuit operators also decide whether their right operand executes. Integer promotions happen before arithmetic on narrow types, and assignment can perform a further conversion afterward. Distinguish a legal conversion with a defined wrap from signed overflow or an unsequenced conflicting access; those latter cases do not supply a portable numeric answer.''',
E('fundamental', 'For initialized ints x=6 and y=3, find x&y and x&&y.', r'Write bit patterns::6=(110)_2,\quad3=(011)_2::The nonzero values have different sets of one bits.~Apply bitwise AND::110\mathbin{\&}011=010::Only the shared middle bit survives, giving two.~Apply logical AND::(6\ne0)\land(3\ne0)=1::Both operands are true, so the logical result is exactly one.', 'x&y=2; x&&y=1.', 'The bitwise result has value two, while every logical-AND result belongs to {0,1}.'),
E('application', 'Trace int x=0,y=5; int z=(x!=0)&&(y/x>1). Does division execute?', r'Evaluate the left operand::x\ne0=0::The comparison is false because x is zero.~Use short circuit::::Logical AND skips its right operand after a false left operand.~Assign the result::z=0::No division occurs, so this expression does not divide by zero.', 'z=0; x=0 and y=5 remain unchanged.', 'Replacing && by bitwise & would evaluate the right-side division and lose this protection.'),
E('trap', 'With eight-bit unsigned char and a 32-bit int, trace unsigned char u=0; u--; Is this signed overflow?', r'Promote the operand::::The arithmetic uses int because it can represent all unsigned-char values.~Compute before conversion::0-1=-1::This int result is representable and does not overflow.~Store in the narrow unsigned type::u=(-1)\bmod256=255::The assignment conversion wraps to the unsigned-char range.', 'u=255; the operation is defined under the supplied widths.', 'Adding one modulo 256 returns zero, confirming the wrap; no out-of-range signed arithmetic was performed.'))

P('3-recursion-calls', 'Recursion', '''A recursive definition becomes an executable algorithm only after its domain, stopping rule and decreasing measure are clear. Track the argument of every child call, not an informal claim that the input becomes smaller. Returned values are combined by the caller after the child finishes; printed effects occur at the exact statement where they are written. Multiple children can represent different subproblems or duplicate the same one. A function's name does not determine its recurrence: changing a child argument changes the mathematics even when the code still terminates. Use small inputs to expose the complete call structure, then check the result against the problem's independent definition.''',
E('fundamental', 'Let digitSum(n)=0 if n=0, otherwise n%10+digitSum(n/10), using nonnegative integer division. Evaluate digitSum(123).', r'Decompose successive inputs::::Calls use 123,12,1,0, retaining final digits 3,2,1 in the callers.~Return the base value::digitSum(0)=0::The descent stops instead of recursing on zero again.~Combine while returning::1+0=1,\quad2+1=3,\quad3+3=6::Each caller adds its saved digit to the returned sum.', 'digitSum(123)=6.', 'Direct addition of the decimal digits 1+2+3 also gives six.'),
E('application', 'Solve three-disk Tower of Hanoi from A to C using B. State the recursive move sequence and move count.', r'Move the smaller pair::::The first two-disk subproblem uses moves A→C, A→B, C→B, freeing disk three.~Move the largest disk::::Move disk three A→C exactly once before solving the second subproblem.~Move the pair onto it::::Use B→A, B→C, A→C. The full sequence has seven moves.', 'Moves: A→C,A→B,C→B,A→C,B→A,B→C,A→C; count 7.', 'The recurrence M(3)=2M(2)+1=2·3+1=7 agrees; every smaller disk stays above larger disks.'),
E('trap', 'A function has f(n)=1 for n≤1, otherwise f(n)=f(n−1)+f(n−1). Evaluate f(3) and decide whether it is Fibonacci.', r'Evaluate the smaller state::f(2)=f(1)+f(1)=2::Both children have argument one.~Evaluate the requested state::f(3)=f(2)+f(2)=4::The caller adds two identical state results.~Check the intended recurrence::::Fibonacci would use distinct arguments n−1 and n−2, so this function solves another recurrence.', 'f(3)=4; this is not the Fibonacci recurrence.', 'For n≥1 this definition gives 2^(n−1), confirming four at n=3 without relying on the function name.'))

P('3-recursion-calls', 'Call stack', '''A call stack records pending work, not merely the argument values currently being visited. Each frame retains its own local variables, return location and information needed after its child finishes. Sketch frames from oldest at the bottom to newest at the top, and remove only the frame that returns. Sequential child calls do not keep both completed subtrees on the stack; after the first child returns, its space can be reused by the second. A recursive call does not reset the caller's local variable or mutate it just because a child uses the same name. Maximum simultaneous frames therefore differs from total invocations and from the final value returned.''',
E('fundamental', 'f(n) stores local=n+10; if(n>0) f(n−1); then prints local. Trace f(2) without frame optimization.', r'Push distinct frames::::The active frames retain local values 12,11,10 for arguments 2,1,0.~Return the deepest call::::The n=0 frame prints ten and is popped, exposing the suspended n=1 frame.~Resume saved work::::The next frames print eleven and twelve using their own unchanged locals.', 'Output is 10,11,12; maximum simultaneous frames is three.', 'There are three independent locals, and unwind order is the reverse of the descent argument order.'),
E('application', 'g(n) calls g(n−1) twice sequentially for n>0 and returns immediately at n=0. For g(2), compare total calls with maximum frames.', r'Count the invocation tree::1+2+4=7::The root has two children, each of which has two base children.~Identify the longest active chain::::A path is g(2)→g(1)→g(0), containing three frames.~Reuse returned space::::The second child starts after the first returns, so completed sibling subtrees are not simultaneously stacked.', 'Seven calls occur, but at most three frames coexist.', 'Counting root-to-leaf length gives three independently of the seven-node call-tree size.'),
E('trap', 'Caller f has local x=4 and calls h; h has local x=9 and returns. With no pointer arguments, what is f\'s x afterward?', "Bind the caller object::::f's x belongs to the still-active caller frame.~Create and remove the child::::h's separate x=9 exists only in h's frame and disappears when h returns.~Resume the caller::::Execution resumes with f's own x=4; equal spellings do not create aliases.", 'The caller still has x=4.', 'No statement supplied h with the address of f\'s object or assigned to that caller object.'))

P('3-recursion-cost', 'Recursive complexity', '''Derive recursive time from the whole invocation tree and recursive space from the maximum live path plus retained allocations. The number and sizes of children determine the recurrence; their execution order determines which frames or buffers coexist. Include base invocations in an exact call count, even if they perform no recursive work. A logarithmic recursion depth does not guarantee logarithmic memory when large buffers remain live. Conversely, exponential invocation count can coexist with only linear stack depth because sibling calls execute sequentially. State whether inputs are powers of two, how small states terminate, and what work the base performs before summing a recursion tree or claiming a tight bound.''',
E('fundamental', 'A procedure makes two calls on n−1 until n=0. Find exact invocations and depth for n=3.', r'Count each level::1,2,4,8::Levels correspond to arguments 3,2,1,0.~Sum invocations::1+2+4+8=15::The eight base invocations still count as calls.~Count active levels::D=4::One root-to-base path contains four calls.', '15 invocations; maximum depth 4 frames.', 'The recurrence C(0)=1, C(n)=1+2C(n−1) gives C(3)=15.'),
E('application', 'For n=8, each nonbase call splits into two halves and performs n units of local work; size-one calls cost one. Find exact total work.', r'Charge internal levels::8+8+8=24::There are three internal levels, and their subproblem sizes sum to eight at each.~Charge leaves::8\cdot1=8::Eight size-one subproblems each contribute one base unit.~Combine the costs::24+8=32::Internal work and base work are separate contributions.', 'Total work is 32 units, with Θ(n log n) growth for powers of two.', 'T(2)=4, T(4)=12 and T(8)=2·12+8=32 reproduce the tree sum.'),
E('trap', 'A halving recursion retains an array of length n in each active frame, then calls on n/2. Is its auxiliary space Θ(log n)?', r'Count depth::::Halving creates logarithmically many frames.~Count retained storage::n+n/2+n/4+\cdots<2n::The parent buffers remain live during the child, so frame sizes are not constant.~Establish the lower bound::S(n)\ge n::The initial buffer alone needs linear space.', 'Auxiliary space is Θ(n), despite Θ(log n) frame depth.', 'For n=8 with lengths 8,4,2,1, live buffers total 15, between n and 2n.'))

P('3-linear-structures-array-insertion', 'Arrays', '''Array updates must preserve the logical sequence as well as stay inside allocated capacity. Distinguish a slot containing a leftover value from an element that belongs to the current sequence. When closing a deletion gap, copy the suffix left from low indices upward; when opening an insertion gap, copy right from high indices downward. The safe direction prevents a source from being overwritten before it is read. A sorted merge into spare capacity can also work backward because the largest remaining value belongs at the latest available destination. Establish the unread-source and completed-destination regions, then count movement from the actual affected suffix rather than assigning constant cost to every indexed operation.''',
E('fundamental', 'Delete zero-based position 1 from the logical array [5,7,9,11], capacity four.', r'Identify the removed slot::::Seven is removed, leaving a gap before the suffix 9,11.~Shift toward the front::::Assign a[1]=9 and then a[2]=11, reading sources before they are overwritten.~Reduce logical length::length=3::The trailing slot is outside the new sequence even if it still contains eleven.', 'Logical array [5,9,11]; two elements moved.', 'Every retained original element appears once in order, and the old final slot is excluded by the new length.'),
E('application', 'Merge sorted B=[2,3,8] into A=[1,4,7] with three spare A slots, writing backward.', r'Fill the largest destinations::::Compare final unread values: place 8 at A[5], then 7 at A[4].~Continue backward::::Place 4 at A[3], then 3 at A[2], then 2 at A[1]. Unread A[0]=1 remains safe.~Finish the merged sequence::::All B values are consumed and A[0] is already the smallest value.', 'A becomes [1,2,3,4,7,8].', 'Each input order is preserved and the output contains the multiset union of the two inputs.'),
E('trap', 'Insert 9 at the front of [1,2,3] with capacity four, but copy a[j+1]=a[j] for j=0,1,2 in increasing order. What goes wrong?', r'Overwrite the next source::::j=0 sets a[1]=1, destroying the original two before it is copied.~Propagate the overwritten value::::j=1 and j=2 copy that same one into slots two and three.~Write the insertion::::Setting a[0]=9 now produces [9,1,1,1], not the required preserved suffix.', 'The incorrect result is [9,1,1,1]; copying rightward must run from the end.', 'Original values two and three disappeared, violating the required permutation-plus-one property.'))

P('3-linear-structures-lists', 'Linked lists', '''Treat a linked-list operation as a change to graph edges between node objects. Before overwriting a link, retain every node or suffix still needed for the operation. The head is an incoming reference too, so deleting or inserting the first node requires updating it instead of an ordinary predecessor field. For reversal, maintain a reversed prefix, the current node and the untouched suffix; saving the next link is what keeps the suffix reachable. Complexity claims depend on supplied references. A known singly linked tail still lacks its incoming predecessor reference, and constant-time deletion by copying a successor cannot handle that tail or preserve arbitrary node identity requirements.''',
E('fundamental', 'Delete the head of singly linked list A→B→C→null, given only head=A.', r'Retain the successor::::Save B from A.next before releasing or disconnecting A.~Redirect the entry::::Set head=B; no predecessor node exists to update.~Remove the old head::::A can now be released under the allocation model while B→C remains reachable.', 'The list is B→C→null, with constant pointer-update work.', 'Following head visits exactly the two retained nodes and never the removed A.'),
E('application', 'Reverse singly linked list 1→2→3→null using prev=null and current=1.', r'Reverse the first link::::Save next=2, set 1.next=null, then prev=1 and current=2.~Reverse the next link::::Save next=3, set 2.next=1, then prev=2 and current=3.~Complete reversal::::Save next=null, set 3.next=2 and set head=3 after current becomes null.', 'The list becomes 3→2→1→null.', 'All three original nodes remain reachable exactly once, and the old head is now the terminating tail.'),
E('trap', 'In a singly linked list of n nodes, only a pointer to its tail is supplied. Can the tail always be deleted in O(1) without changing other node identities?', r'Find the missing information::::The tail has no next node and no stored predecessor link.~Reject the successor-copy trick::::There is no successor whose contents and link could be copied into the tail.~Account for the incoming link::::With a head available, locating the predecessor may require traversing n−1 nodes; without it deletion may be impossible.', 'No universal O(1) deletion follows from a tail pointer alone.', 'A doubly linked node would supply a predecessor link, demonstrating the representation assumption missing here.'))

P('3-linear-structures-stacks', 'Stacks', '''A stack models pending work whose newest unresolved item must be handled first. Write the entire stack with its top convention when tracing, because an unordered set cannot capture operand order or nesting. Parenthesis checking needs symbol types as well as counts; each closer must match the most recent unmatched opener. Expression conversion uses precedence to decide which operators remain pending, while evaluation uses the right-then-left pop order. Before every pop, establish that a value exists. A malformed expression can have balanced final size yet require an earlier underflow, so validate the sequence of operations rather than only counting net pushes and pops.''',
E('fundamental', 'Use a stack to test whether the bracket string ([)] is properly nested.', r'Push openers::::After ( then [, the stack from bottom to top is [(,[].~Compare the first closer::::The closer ) requires a top (, but the actual top is [.~Reject immediately::::The mismatch proves invalid nesting even though total counts of each bracket type balance.', 'The string is not properly nested.', 'The crossing pair cannot be closed in last-opened-first order; equal counts alone therefore fail as a correctness test.'),
E('application', 'Convert infix 3+4*2 to postfix with an operator stack, then evaluate it.', r'Save the pending operators::::Output 3 then 4; the stack contains + then * because multiplication has higher precedence.~Finish the operand and drain::::Output 2, then pop * and +, producing postfix 3 4 2 * +.~Evaluate in stack order::4\cdot2=8,\quad3+8=11::The multiplication result is pushed before addition consumes it.', 'Postfix is 3 4 2 * +; value is 11.', 'Direct precedence-aware infix evaluation gives 3+(4·2)=11, matching the stack computation.'),
E('trap', 'Evaluate postfix 5 2 −. Why does popping the first value as the left operand fail?', "Push operands::::The stack is [5,2] with two on top.~Assign operand roles::right=2,\\quad left=5::The top was pushed later and belongs on the operator's right.~Apply subtraction::left-right=5-2=3::Reversing the roles would compute a different expression.", 'The value is 3; using first pop as left incorrectly gives −3.', 'The postfix order corresponds to infix 5−2, not 2−5.'))

P('3-linear-structures-queues', 'Queues', '''A FIFO queue preserves arrival order even when its physical storage or implementation changes. Keep a logical front-to-rear sequence separate from internal arrays or stacks. A queue built from two stacks accumulates new arrivals in an input stack and removes older records from an output stack. Transfer input to output only when output is empty, reversing the current batch exactly once. Each item can be charged for one input push, one transfer pair and one output pop, giving constant amortized operations although a particular dequeue can move many items. Empty-state handling matters because removing an absent item is not a legitimate FIFO operation or a numerical return value.''',
E('fundamental', 'Starting empty, enqueue A, enqueue B, dequeue, then enqueue C. State the removed value and queue order.', r'Record arrivals::::The front-to-rear sequence after two enqueues is [A,B].~Remove the oldest::::Dequeue removes A and leaves [B].~Append the later arrival::::Enqueuing C gives [B,C], with B still ahead of C.', 'Removed A; remaining order B,C.', 'Dequeuing the remaining queue would yield B then C, exactly their arrival order.'),
E('application', 'Implement a queue with input/output stacks, top at the right. Enqueue 1,2; dequeue; enqueue 3; dequeue twice. Show transfers.', r'Transfer the first batch::::Input [1,2] becomes output [2,1] when the first dequeue finds output empty; pop one.~Preserve the older output::::Enqueue three into input [3]. The next dequeue pops two from output without transferring three.~Transfer after output empties::::Move three to output and pop it on the final dequeue.', 'Dequeue results are 1,2,3, with two batch transfers.', 'Every item crosses from input to output once, so three items need linear total stack operations despite a multi-item first transfer.'),
E('trap', 'A queue starts empty, receives X, then is dequeued twice. Is the second result another X from the old storage slot?', r'Consume the only arrival::::The first dequeue removes X and makes the logical size zero.~Check the second precondition::::The next dequeue has no live queue element to remove.~Separate storage from membership::::An old byte pattern in an array slot does not represent a current enqueued item.', 'The second dequeue must report underflow or an empty result under the API.', 'Successful dequeues cannot exceed successful enqueues for an initially empty queue.'))

P('3-linear-structures-queues', 'Circular queue', '''A circular buffer reuses released slots by reducing index updates modulo physical capacity. First specify whether rear means last occupied or next free, and whether fullness uses a count, a flag or one reserved slot. These choices change both formulas and usable capacity. Under a count convention, all physical slots are usable and equal head/rear indices can describe either empty or full. Under a reserved-slot convention, equality means empty and the next rear position colliding with head means full. Trace the logical sequence alongside indices, because numerical index order does not describe FIFO order after wrapping. Test capacity before writes and size before reads.''',
E('fundamental', 'Capacity three, count convention, head=rear=0 and empty. Enqueue P,Q,R, then dequeue once; rear is next free.', r'Fill each slot::rear=(0+3)\bmod3=0::P,Q,R occupy slots 0,1,2 and count becomes three.~Recognize full equality::::head and rear both equal zero, but count distinguishes fullness from emptiness.~Remove the front::head=(0+1)\bmod3=1::P is removed, rear stays zero and count becomes two.', 'Removed P; head=1, rear=0, count=2; remaining order Q,R.', 'The occupied slots are now 1 and 2, matching two remaining arrivals.'),
E('application', 'Reserved-slot buffer has physical capacity four, head=3, next-free rear=1. Enqueue X, then dequeue. Find final indices and intermediate fullness.', r'Identify occupied slots::size=(1-3+4)\bmod4=2::Slots three and zero hold the older two records.~Append and test fullness::rear=(1+1)\bmod4=2::X enters slot one; next rear would be three, equal to head, so the buffer is full.~Remove the oldest::head=(3+1)\bmod4=0::The dequeue frees slot three; rear remains two.', 'After enqueue the buffer is full; afterward head=0, rear=2 with two elements.', 'Logical occupied positions zero and one contain the retained old record then X; usable capacity is three.'),
E('trap', 'A count-based capacity-five queue has head=tail=2. Can equality alone determine whether an enqueue is allowed?', r'Construct the empty case::count=0::Equal indices can describe no records, so enqueue would be allowed.~Construct the full case::count=5::Five arrivals without removals wrap tail back to head, so enqueue must be rejected.~Use the missing state::::Read count or an equivalent full flag; index equality supplies insufficient information.', 'No; the same equal indices can mean empty or full.', 'The two cases have identical indices but different available capacities, disproving an equality-only rule.'))

P('3-trees-bst-tree-height-shape', 'Trees', '''Tree counting becomes easier when each quantity is tied to a structural invariant. Every nonroot vertex has one incoming parent edge, giving n−1 edges regardless of branching pattern. For a full binary tree, counting child edges also gives twice the number of internal vertices, which relates leaves to internal vertices. Height bounds depend on whether the tree is arbitrary, complete or perfect: an arbitrary binary tree may be a chain, whereas completeness constrains the occupied positions. Always declare the edge-based or vertex-based height convention. To classify a shape, inspect missing child positions as well as child counts; being full and being complete are independent requirements.''',
E('fundamental', 'A full binary tree has three internal vertices. How many leaves and total vertices does it have?', r'Count child edges::|E|=2I=6::Each internal vertex contributes exactly two child edges.~Use the tree edge invariant::n-1=6\Rightarrow n=7::Every nonroot vertex contributes one parent edge.~Separate leaves::L=n-I=7-3=4::The total consists of internal vertices plus leaves.', 'Four leaves and seven vertices.', 'The full-binary identity L=I+1 gives the same four leaves.'),
E('application', 'A complete binary tree has edge-based height three. Give the minimum and maximum possible number of vertices.', r'Fill earlier levels::1+2+4=7::Completeness requires every level before the final one to be full.~Require the stated height::n_{min}=7+1=8::At least one vertex must occur on level three.~Fill the final level::n_{max}=7+8=15::That level has at most eight positions.', 'The vertex count lies between 8 and 15 inclusive.', 'The equivalent inequality 2^3≤n≤2^4−1 matches the counted levels.'),
E('trap', 'Root A has children B,C, and B has only a left child D. Is this binary tree complete and full?', r'Inspect level placement::::Levels zero and one are full; level two occupies only its leftmost position D.~Apply completeness::::The final level has no gap before an occupied position, so the tree is complete.~Count children per vertex::::B has exactly one child, violating the full-tree requirement of zero or two.', 'It is complete but not full.', 'Level-order array positions are A,B,C,D with no hole, while B provides a direct counterexample to fullness.'))

P('3-trees-bst-bst', 'Binary search trees', '''The BST invariant constrains every descendant, not merely a node's immediate children. Maintain an allowed key interval while descending: entering a left subtree tightens the upper limit, and entering a right subtree tightens the lower limit. Insertion adds a leaf at the first empty link on that comparison path. Two-child deletion replaces the key by a successor or predecessor, then removes that replacement from its original location, potentially reconnecting its one child. State a duplicate policy before tracing equal comparisons. Search cost follows height, so a legal BST can still perform linear work when insertions produce a chain. Verify an update by its full inorder sequence and retained key multiset.''',
E('fundamental', 'Insert distinct keys 7,3,9,5 into an empty ordinary BST, then search for five.', "Create the upper shape::::Seven becomes root; three attaches left and nine attaches right.~Place the fourth key::::Five is smaller than seven and larger than three, so it becomes three's right child.~Follow the search path::::Comparisons visit seven, then three, then five, where the match succeeds.", 'The search path is 7→3→5; inorder is 3,5,7,9.', 'The inorder sequence is sorted and includes each inserted key exactly once.'),
E('application', 'Delete root eight using its successor. Left child is three; right child is twelve, whose left child is ten with right child eleven.', "Find the true successor::::The minimum in the right subtree is ten, reached by going left from twelve.~Replace the root key::::Store ten at the root, preserving the root's original two subtree links.~Remove the old ten::::Reconnect twelve's left link to eleven, the old successor's only child.", 'Root ten has left three and right twelve; twelve has left eleven.', 'Inorder is 3,10,11,12. Removing eight and retaining eleven confirms both ordering and child reconnection.'),
E('trap', 'Insert 1,2,3,4,5 into an ordinary unbalanced BST. Is finding five guaranteed logarithmic?', r'Follow successive insertions::::Every new key exceeds all earlier ones, so each becomes a right child of the previous key.~Measure shape::h=4::Five vertices form a chain with four edges.~Count the search::::Searching for five visits all five vertices; in general sorted insertion can require n comparisons.', 'No; this tree has linear-height and linear worst-case search.', 'Its inorder is still sorted, showing that the BST invariant does not itself imply balance.'))

P('3-trees-bst-trees', 'Tree traversals', '''A traversal order is determined by when a node is emitted relative to recursive child visits. Label the visit moment before, between or after the child calls for preorder, inorder or postorder. Missing children contribute no labels but still affect reconstruction choices. For distinct keys, preorder supplies each subtree's root and inorder partitions its left and right descendants, so together they determine a unique binary tree. Preorder and postorder lack the left-versus-right distinction for a single-child node. Reconstruction requires more than matching vertex counts: verify the proposed shape by regenerating both supplied traversals. Do not assume inorder is sorted unless a separate BST invariant is present.''',
E('fundamental', 'R has left child L and right child M; M has left child N. Compute all three depth-first traversal orders.', r'Emit on entry::::Preorder visits R before L, then M before N, giving R,L,M,N.~Emit between children::::Inorder finishes L, emits R, then visits N before M, giving L,R,N,M.~Emit on return::::Postorder emits L, then N,M, then R, giving L,N,M,R.', 'Preorder R L M N; inorder L R N M; postorder L N M R.', 'Each order contains the same four labels exactly once, and M/N appear according to the stated visit moment.'),
E('application', 'Reconstruct a distinct-label tree from preorder A,B,D,E,C and inorder D,B,E,A,C; give its postorder.', r'Partition at the root::::Preorder starts with A; inorder puts D,B,E on its left and C on its right.~Reconstruct the left subtree::::Its preorder root is B, with D left and E right according to the inorder partition.~Emit completed subtrees::::Postorder visits D,E,B, then C, then A.', 'A has left B and right C; B has children D,E. Postorder is D,E,B,C,A.', 'Regenerating preorder gives A,B,D,E,C and inorder gives D,B,E,A,C, matching both inputs.'),
E('trap', 'Preorder is A,B and postorder is B,A. Must B be A\'s left child?', r'Construct a left-child tree::::A with left child B produces preorder A,B and postorder B,A.~Construct a right-child tree::::A with right child B produces those same two orders.~Find the missing distinction::::Their inorders differ: B,A versus A,B, so the supplied orders do not choose a side.', 'No; both child placements are possible.', 'Two distinct trees satisfying both inputs are a constructive proof of nonuniqueness.'))

P('3-heaps-graphs-heap-bottom-up-build', 'Binary heaps', '''A heap is a priority structure with a shape invariant and an ordering invariant. Both must survive insertion and removal: append or remove only the last array position to preserve completeness, then repair ordering along the affected path. In a min-heap, compare both children before sifting down; in a max-heap, use the larger child instead. A heap's root gives an extreme value, but no global order is promised between unrelated vertices. Keeping a small min-heap can represent the largest k values of a stream, because its root is the least competitive retained candidate. State tie handling and whether the question concerns a fixed heap or repeated online updates.''',
E('fundamental', 'Extract the maximum from max-heap [10,7,8,2,3,4]. Show root replacement and repair.', r'Remove the extreme::::Return ten and move final value four to the root, leaving [4,7,8,2,3].~Choose the larger child::::Eight is larger than seven, so swap the root with the child at index two.~Finish at the leaf::::The moved four has no children in the active array, yielding [8,7,4,2,3].', 'Extracted 10; remaining heap [8,7,4,2,3].', 'Eight dominates seven and four; seven dominates two and three. Completeness follows from deleting the final slot.'),
E('application', 'Maintain the largest three stream values using a size-three min-heap for arrivals 4,1,7,2,9. Show replacements.', r'Fill the retained set::::The first three arrivals form min-heap [1,4,7].~Replace the smallest retained value::::Two exceeds root one, so replacement yields [2,4,7].~Repair after nine arrives::::Replace root two by nine and sift with four, producing [4,9,7].', 'The retained values are 4,7,9; the third-largest stream value is 4.', 'Sorting the whole stream gives 1,2,4,7,9, whose largest three agree with the heap contents.'),
E('trap', 'Is min-heap [1,8,2,9,10,3,4] invalid because sibling eight exceeds sibling two?', r'Check the root::::One is no larger than either eight or two.~Check lower parents::::Eight is no larger than nine and ten; two is no larger than three and four.~Reject a false extra invariant::::Heap order compares parents with children, not siblings or the whole array sequence.', 'It is a valid min-heap despite being globally unsorted.', 'All six parent-child inequalities hold; the sibling pair supplies no heap violation.'))

P('3-heaps-graphs-directed-graph-degrees', 'Graphs', '''A graph statement needs a precise model before degree or connectivity formulas are applied. Directed arcs contribute separately to source outdegree and destination indegree; undirected edges contribute one incidence at each end, with a loop counted twice toward undirected degree. Parallel edges matter to counts even when reachability is unchanged. A weakly connected directed graph can fail strong connectivity because orientation blocks return routes. Draw or tabulate the supplied edges, including isolated vertices, then trace permitted directions rather than inferring reachability from degree totals. The degree-sum identities verify incidence accounting, while actual routes or component searches establish connectivity and cycles.''',
E('fundamental', 'Undirected vertices P,Q,R have edges P–Q and Q–R. Compute degrees and verify the edge count.', r'Count endpoint incidences::::P meets one edge, Q meets two, and R meets one.~Sum degrees::1+2+1=4::Every undirected edge was counted at both endpoints.~Recover the edge count::|E|=4/2=2::The handshaking identity matches the supplied edge list.', 'Degrees are 1,2,1 and there are two edges.', 'The adjacency lengths [1,2,1] provide the same incidence totals.'),
E('application', 'Directed graph A→B, B→C, C→A, C→D has four vertices. Is it weakly connected or strongly connected?', r'Inspect the underlying edges::::Ignoring orientation joins A,B,C,D in one component.~Establish the directed core::::A,B,C reach one another through their directed cycle.~Test the final vertex::::D has no outgoing arc, so it cannot return to A or reach the cycle.', 'The graph is weakly connected but not strongly connected.', 'The explicit failure D→A refutes strong connectivity, while the undirected path D–C–B–A verifies weak connectivity.'),
E('trap', 'An undirected graph has two parallel A–B edges and one loop at A. What are degrees A,B and the degree sum?', r'Count parallel incidences::::Each A–B edge adds one to each endpoint, giving two at A and two at B.~Count the loop correctly::::The loop contributes two incidences to A, not one.~Check the identity::deg(A)+deg(B)=4+2=6=2\cdot3::All three edges are accounted for.', 'deg(A)=4 and deg(B)=2.', 'Counting the loop once would give an odd total five, contradicting the undirected degree-sum invariant.'))

P('3-heaps-graphs-adjacency-list-build', 'Adjacency list', '''Adjacency lists represent stored incidences rather than all possible vertex pairs. For directed input, outgoing lists contain one entry per arc; for undirected input, the same logical edge normally appears at both endpoints. Neighbor order is part of a trace when discovery order is requested, but it is not an additional graph edge. To remove an undirected edge, repair both endpoint lists. Membership cost depends on list lengths unless extra indexing is provided, whereas enumeration touches only actual neighbors. Include empty headers and parallel entries under the stated graph model. Compare traversal work using the total number of entries rather than assuming E always equals that total.''',
E('fundamental', 'Build undirected adjacency lists for vertices 1,2,3 and edges 1–2,1–3, listing neighbors increasingly.', "Add the first edge at both ends::::The edge 1–2 adds neighbor 2 to vertex 1's list and neighbor 1 to vertex 2's list.~Add the second edge at both ends::::The edge 1–3 adds neighbor 3 to vertex 1's list and neighbor 1 to vertex 3's list.~Read all lists::::The lists are 1:[2,3], 2:[1], 3:[1].", 'The representation has three headers and four neighbor entries.', 'The entry total is 2E=4, and every undirected incidence has its reverse entry.'),
E('application', 'Directed lists are A:[B,D], B:[C], C:[], D:[C]. Enumerate reachable vertices from B and count scanned entries.', r'Start from B::::Mark B and inspect its one outgoing entry C.~Expand C::::Its list is empty, so the search discovers nothing further.~Separate unvisited headers::::A and D are unreachable from B and their outgoing entries are not scanned by this single-source search.', 'Reachable set {B,C}; one neighbor entry is scanned.', 'The only directed route starting at B is B→C; incoming arcs to B do not permit a reverse move to A.'),
E('trap', 'Undirected lists are A:[B], B:[A,C], C:[B]. Delete edge B–C by removing only C from B\'s list. Is the representation consistent?', "Remove the first incidence::::B becomes [A], so its list no longer describes an edge to C.~Inspect the reverse incidence::::C still contains B, claiming the same undirected edge exists.~Repair both endpoints::::Remove B from C's list as well, making C empty.", 'No; both reverse entries must be removed.', 'After proper deletion the total entries fall from four to two, exactly twice the remaining one edge.'))

P('3-heaps-graphs-adjacency-matrix-build', 'Adjacency matrix', '''A matrix needs a fixed vertex order and an explicit meaning for every entry. Boolean entries can record simple edge existence, while counts can preserve parallel-edge multiplicity and weights need an unambiguous absent-edge marker. Matrix rows enable direct membership tests, but enumerating a sparse vertex still scans all columns. Undirected symmetry is a verification rule only under an undirected model. For a weighted shortest-path initialization, a zero diagonal represents the empty route while a genuine negative loop must remain distinguishable. Separate graph adjacency from distance closure: a matrix entry produced by a multi-edge route is not a newly supplied original edge.''',
E('fundamental', 'For undirected path X–Y–Z with order X,Y,Z, write the adjacency matrix and test X–Z.', r'Initialize zero entries::::There are nine ordered-pair cells and no self-loops.~Set symmetric pairs::M=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&1\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}::Each undirected edge changes two mirrored cells.~Read the requested pair::M_{XZ}=0::X and Z have a route via Y but no direct edge.', 'The displayed matrix represents the path; X–Z is absent.', 'Symmetry and four off-diagonal ones agree with two undirected edges.'),
E('application', 'A 500-vertex matrix is used to enumerate all outgoing neighbors of each vertex, although the directed graph has only 700 edges. How many cells are inspected?', r'Count one row::500::A matrix row includes a position for every possible destination.~Count all rows::500\cdot500=250000::All vertices require all their row positions inspected.~Compare stored sparsity::::Seven hundred actual edges change how many cells are nonzero, not how many positions are scanned.', '250,000 cells; Θ(V²) enumeration work.', 'Summing 500 scans over 500 rows independently yields the same count.'),
E('trap', 'Weighted matrix uses zero for absent edges, but graph contains edge A→B of weight zero. Can a reader distinguish it from no edge?', r'Encode the two cases::::Both a missing pair and this valid zero-weight edge receive the same value zero.~Attempt a membership test::::A test based solely on that cell cannot select the correct case.~Repair the representation::::Use an additional presence bit or a distinct sentinel such as infinity for absence.', 'No; the encoding is ambiguous.', 'Two different graphs map to the same matrix cell, so no decoding rule can recover both correctly.'))

P('4-analysis-worst-case-input', 'Asymptotic worst case time', '''A worst-case claim needs both a universal upper bound and inputs that make the algorithm approach that bound. Identify the primitive being counted, because successful comparisons, loop-condition checks and assignments have different exact totals. Input size must match the data representation; a numeric capacity is not interchangeable with its binary bit length. Structural worst cases depend on the actual policy: a sorted input is harmful to a first-pivot quicksort but useful to some other algorithms. When a loop stops early, maximize the executed prefix instead of blindly multiplying declared bounds. Separate the finite exact maximum from its eventual growth class, and avoid interpreting a loose upper bound as a tight result.''',
E('fundamental', 'For i=1..n, an inner loop executes j=1..i with one operation. Derive the worst-case operation count.', r'Count each row::::The inner counts are 1,2,3 through n; no data-dependent exit changes them.~Sum the rows::T(n)=n(n+1)/2::All rows execute, so this is also the maximum for size n.~Bound the growth::n^2/2\le T(n)\le n^2::For n≥1 these matching quadratic bounds establish tight order.', 'Exactly n(n+1)/2 operations; Θ(n²) worst-case time.', 'At n=3 the explicit rows 1+2+3 total six, matching the formula.'),
E('application', 'Inclusive binary search on fifteen sorted distinct keys returns immediately on equality. What is the maximum number of midpoint comparisons?', r'Build the deepest candidate path::::The largest remaining lengths are 15,7,3,1 under halving.~Count tested midpoints::C_{max}=4::A target at a deepest leaf, or an appropriate absent target, requires one comparison per listed length.~Translate the pattern::C_{max}=\lfloor\log_2 15\rfloor+1::The formula gives four under this convention.', 'Four midpoint comparisons.', 'A fifteen-node balanced decision tree has four levels including its root, consistent with the explicit length trace.'),
E('trap', 'An algorithm executes exactly n operations. A claim says its worst-case time is O(n²), therefore it must take quadratic time. Assess the conclusion.', r'Identify the actual function::T_{worst}(n)=n::No input changes the supplied count.~Check the loose upper bound::n\le n^2\quad(n\ge1)::The O(n²) statement is true as an upper bound.~Check tightness::T_{worst}(n)/n^2=1/n\to0::No fixed positive quadratic lower factor holds.', 'O(n²) is true, but tight worst-case growth is Θ(n).', 'The stronger bound O(n) and lower bound Ω(n) coincide, disproving the inferred quadratic requirement.'))

P('4-analysis-space', 'Space complexity', '''Peak space measures objects live at the same instant. Draw allocation and release intervals rather than adding every allocation that occurs during the run. The convention for input and output storage should be explicit: total space may include them, whereas auxiliary space usually describes extra working memory. A fixed set of indices can operate on a large supplied array without copying it. Recursive algorithms add frame storage even when their array updates are in place, and retained buffers can dominate those frames. Shared references do not duplicate the referenced data, but a materialized slice does. Prove both a largest simultaneous allocation requirement and a matching upper bound before giving a tight class.''',
E('fundamental', 'Transpose an n×n matrix in place by swapping a[i][j] with a[j][i] for j>i, using three loop/index variables and one temporary.', r'Keep the supplied input::::The matrix already exists and is not copied by the procedure.~Locate live working values::::Only i,j,n and one temporary element are needed regardless of matrix dimension.~State the convention::S_{aux}=\Theta(1)::Excluding the supplied matrix, working storage is constant.', 'Constant auxiliary space; the input matrix itself occupies Θ(n²).', 'Each off-diagonal pair is exchanged using the same temporary, whose storage is reused rather than accumulated.'),
E('application', 'Balanced merge sort reuses one n-element merge buffer and has logarithmic recursion depth. Find peak auxiliary space.', r'Count the shared buffer::S_{buffer}=n::One allocation serves all merges rather than one fresh retained n-buffer per level.~Count active frames::S_{frames}=O(\log n)::Balanced recursive descent retains logarithmically many constant-sized frames.~Combine live requirements::n+O(\log n)=\Theta(n)::The buffer dominates while also supplying a linear lower bound.', 'Θ(n) auxiliary space.', 'Even at maximum depth, adding logarithmic frame state cannot exceed the linear buffer order.'),
E('trap', 'An algorithm scans n input records with one index and writes n output records. It claims both auxiliary and total space are O(1). Assess this when output storage counts toward total space.', r'Identify working state::::A streaming scan can use constant temporary state.~Account for required output::S_{output}=n::The completed n-record result must occupy linear storage under the stated convention.~Separate the measures::::Excluding input/output gives constant auxiliary work storage; including output makes total space at least linear.', 'Auxiliary space can be O(1); total space is Θ(n) if input/output records are counted.', 'The result alone supplies n simultaneously retained records, so constant total space is impossible here.'))

P('4-analysis-bounds', 'Big O', '''Big O is a quantified eventual upper bound. Choose positive constants c and n₀ so that the measured nonnegative function never exceeds c times the comparison function after n₀. One function can satisfy many upper bounds, and the tightest familiar one is usually the useful answer when a question asks complexity. Derive the operation function first; a nested loop with geometrically changing limits is not automatically quadratic. Logarithm bases differ only by constant factors for fixed bases greater than one. Check growth ratios or explicit inequalities to establish bounds, and never replace an upper-bound relation with equality of growth without also proving a corresponding lower bound.''',
E('fundamental', 'Prove T(n)=2n log₂n+3n is O(n²) for integers n≥2.', r'Bound the logarithm::\log_2 n\le n::This valid inequality supplies a simple upper estimate.~Substitute::T(n)\le2n^2+3n^2=5n^2::For n≥2, the remaining linear term is also bounded quadratically.~Choose witnesses::c=5,\quad n_0=2::These constants satisfy the eventual upper-bound definition.', 'T(n) is O(n²); this is a valid loose bound.', 'Its tighter Θ(n log n) growth does not contradict the proved quadratic upper bound.'),
E('application', 'For n=2^k, a loop uses i=1,2,4,... while i<n and performs i operations at each value. Give a useful Big O bound.', r'List the performed blocks::1,2,4,\ldots,n/2::The iteration count is logarithmic but block sizes grow.~Sum their work::1+2+\cdots+n/2=n-1::A geometric sum replaces multiplication by n at every iteration.~Bound uniformly::n-1\le n::Choosing c=1 gives an O(n) upper bound on the counted operations.', 'Total operations n−1, hence O(n).', 'For n=8 the blocks 1,2,4 total seven, matching the derived function.'),
E('trap', 'T(n)=100n is O(n²). Does that mean T(n) is Θ(n²)?', r'Exhibit an upper bound::100n\le n^2\quad(n\ge100)::The supplied O relation is valid.~Test the missing lower bound::100n/n^2=100/n\to0::No positive constant fraction of n² bounds T from below eventually.~Give tight growth::T(n)=\Theta(n)::Upper and lower linear factors are both exactly one hundred.', 'No; the quadratic upper bound is loose.', 'For increasingly large n, doubling n doubles T rather than quadrupling it.'))

P('4-analysis-bounds', 'Theta', '''Theta expresses matching eventual upper and lower growth bounds for one specified function. Both inequalities must hold beyond a common threshold; testing several small inputs or identifying the largest written term is not by itself a proof. Summations often expose the needed bounds directly, as with dependent nested loops. A function that behaves differently on even and odd sizes may fail a proposed tight class even though it has a valid upper bound and a weaker lower bound. State the function's domain and require positive constants. Theta does not specify an exact count, nor does it imply that best, average and worst cases all share that class.''',
E('fundamental', 'Establish a tight bound for T(n)=5n+11 on integers n≥1.', r'Keep a positive lower term::T(n)\ge5n::The additive eleven cannot reduce the linear contribution.~Bound the constant by a linear term::11\le11n::This holds for all n≥1.~Combine witnesses::5n\le T(n)\le16n::Both positive constant factors apply from the same threshold.', 'T(n)=Θ(n).', 'The ratio T(n)/n=5+11/n stays between five and sixteen and approaches five.'),
E('application', 'An algorithm compares every pair (i,j) with 1≤j≤i≤n. Prove its comparison count is Θ(n²).', r'Derive the exact count::C(n)=\sum_{i=1}^n i=n(n+1)/2::Row i contributes i comparisons.~Bound below::C(n)\ge n^2/2::The additional linear term is nonnegative.~Bound above::C(n)\le n^2\quad(n\ge1)::Using n≤n² produces a matching quadratic factor.', 'C(n)=Θ(n²).', 'At n=4 the ten explicit comparisons lie between eight and sixteen, consistent with the bounds.'),
E('trap', 'Let T(n)=n² for even n and T(n)=n for odd n. Is T(n)=Θ(n²)?', r'Confirm the upper bound::T(n)\le n^2::Both branches satisfy the quadratic upper bound.~Examine odd sizes::T(n)/n^2=1/n::Along arbitrarily large odd n, the ratio tends to zero.~Reject uniform lower witnesses::::Any proposed positive quadratic lower factor fails on a sufficiently large odd size.', 'No. It is O(n²) and Ω(n), but not Θ(n²).', 'Even sizes also prevent Θ(n), so no single argument based on only one branch establishes either proposed tight class.'))

P('4-analysis-bounds', 'Omega', '''Omega supplies an eventual lower bound on a chosen running-time function, and it does not inherently refer to best case. Lower bounds can come from compulsory output, inspection requirements or a decision tree with enough distinguishable outcomes. State the computational model: a comparison-sort lower bound does not prohibit faster methods with restricted integer keys. An algorithm-specific worst-case lower bound may be witnessed by one hard input family, whereas a best-case lower bound must hold even on the easiest legal inputs. Distinguish these quantifiers before translating a statement. To refute a claimed lower bound, find arbitrarily large sizes where the function-to-bound ratio becomes too small.''',
E('fundamental', 'Why must an algorithm finding the maximum of an arbitrary unsorted n-element array inspect every element in the worst case?', r'Assume an unread location::::Suppose the algorithm finishes while some array element has never been inspected.~Change only that location::::An adversary can place a value larger than the reported maximum there without altering any observed value.~Derive compulsory work::T_{worst}(n)=\Omega(n)::Correctness on all arrays requires inspecting all n candidates.', 'The worst-case inspection work is Ω(n).', 'A single linear scan attains O(n), matching the unavoidable inspection order.'),
E('application', 'A deterministic comparison sort handles four distinct keys. Derive a lower bound on its worst-case number of binary comparisons.', r'Count possible orders::4!=24::Each distinct input ordering requires a distinguishable sorted permutation.~Count tree leaves::2^h\ge24::A binary comparison tree of worst-case depth h must distinguish all orders.~Solve the integer bound::h\ge\lceil\log_2 24\rceil=5::Four comparisons give only sixteen outcomes.', 'At least five comparisons in the worst case.', 'The capacity check 2^4=16<24≤32=2^5 independently confirms the threshold.'),
E('trap', 'Linear search has worst-case Ω(n). Must every successful search use at least a linear number of comparisons?', r'Identify the quantified function::::The bound concerns the maximum comparison count among inputs of size n.~Construct an easy input::::Place the target in the first slot, so search returns after one comparison for every n.~Separate cases::::This constant best-case family does not affect the linear maximum attained by absent targets.', 'No; a first-slot match takes one comparison.', 'The same algorithm has best-case Θ(1) and worst-case Θ(n), so the two functions must not be conflated.'))

P('4-search-sort-search', 'Searching', '''Search is the task of identifying whether a desired key satisfies a membership or position condition in a representation. The permitted technique depends on guarantees such as sorted order, direct indexing and whether duplicates require the first occurrence. Linear scanning handles arbitrary order, while two sorted streams can be searched together by advancing the smaller current key. State what a successful result means and what an absent result encodes. Count comparisons along the actual path rather than a generic bound. A method that discards candidates needs a justified invariant; if an assumption is missing, it may skip a valid target even while executing perfectly valid array accesses.''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the first six in unsorted [4,6,6,1] using left-to-right linear search.', r'Inspect the first slot::::Four differs from six, so continue to index one.~Inspect the next slot::::The key at index one is six, so the first-match contract is satisfied.~Stop immediately::::Later duplicate six at index two is not examined and cannot be an earlier match.', 'Return index 1 after two equality comparisons.', 'All preceding indices were checked and differed, establishing the first-occurrence requirement.'),
E('application', 'Find the intersection of sorted distinct lists [1,3,5,7] and [2,3,7] with two advancing indices.', r'Advance smaller fronts::::Compare 1 with 2 and advance the first list; compare 3 with 2 and advance the second.~Record the first equality::::The fronts both equal three; output it and advance both.~Finish the scan::::Compare 5 with 7 and advance the first; then output matching sevens and advance both to an end.', 'Intersection [3,7], using five front comparisons.', 'Checking each reported value in both lists confirms membership; discarded smaller fronts could not match any later larger front.'),
E('trap', 'Apply sorted-array binary-search decisions to unsorted [9,1,7,3,5], seeking nine. Show why it can miss a present key.', r'Inspect the first midpoint::::Index two contains seven, so the sorted-order rule moves low to three.~Discard the actual target::::That move excludes index zero, even though it contains nine; unsorted order invalidates the inference.~Finish the wrong branch::::The later values three and five remain smaller, so the routine eventually reports absence.', 'The routine can falsely report nine absent.', 'A direct scan finds nine at index zero, supplying a counterexample to the discarded-range assumption.'))

P('4-search-sort-sorting-stability', 'Sorting', '''A sorting specification includes key order, record preservation and any stability requirement. Label records so equal keys and their original relative positions remain distinguishable. Different algorithms establish sortedness by different invariants: selection sort fixes a smallest remaining key, while stable multi-pass sorting composes orders by treating the later pass as the primary key. Comparison lower bounds assume arbitrary orderings determined by comparisons; bounded integer keys allow counting-based techniques outside that model. When verifying a result, separately test nondecreasing keys, the exact input multiset and required equal-key order. A fast method can still be incorrect for the requested tie contract, and a stable method may use extra storage.''',
E('fundamental', 'Selection-sort [3,1,2] by putting the smallest remaining value at each position.', r'Fix the first position::::The minimum of the entire suffix is one, so swap it with three to get [1,3,2].~Fix the next position::::The minimum of [3,2] is two, so swap to get [1,2,3].~Verify the fixed prefix::::No remaining suffix exists after the final singleton, and both earlier chosen minima are final.', 'Sorted array [1,2,3].', 'The output is nondecreasing and contains one copy of each original value.'),
E('application', 'Records A=(grade2,id1), B=(grade1,id3), C=(grade2,id2), D=(grade1,id1) must sort by grade then id. Use two stable passes.', r'Sort the secondary key first::::Stable id sorting gives A,D,C,B, with A before D on their equal id.~Sort the primary key second::::Stable grade sorting moves D,B before A,C while preserving id order inside each grade group.~Read the compound order::::The result is D,B,A,C, whose grade-one ids are 1,3 and grade-two ids are 1,2.', 'Two passes yield D,B,A,C.', 'Lexicographic comparison of each adjacent (grade,id) pair confirms the required compound ordering.'),
E('trap', 'Does the Ω(n log n) comparison-sort bound prohibit linear sorting of [3,1,3,0,2] when all keys lie in {0,1,2,3}?', r'Use the stronger key guarantee::::The four possible keys permit direct counting instead of pairwise order comparisons.~Count occurrences::::The counts for keys zero through three are [1,1,1,2].~Emit by key::::Expanding the counts gives [0,1,2,3,3] with O(n+4) work.', 'No; counting sort can be linear for this bounded key range.', 'The output preserves the input multiplicities, and the algorithm is outside the comparison-only lower-bound model.'))

P('4-search-sort-search', 'Binary search', '''Binary search maintains a candidate interval justified by monotone order. Fix inclusive or half-open endpoints before computing updates. For inclusive bounds, a strictly smaller midpoint value removes indices through the midpoint, and a strictly larger value removes indices from the midpoint onward. A first-occurrence search records equality but continues into the left range instead of returning immediately. Termination needs strict interval shrinkage on every nonreturning iteration, including a one- or two-element interval. Compute the midpoint in a way that avoids bounded-integer addition overflow. Verify a result using both equality at the reported index and any extra boundary condition the question requires.''',
E('fundamental', 'Search for seven in sorted [1,4,6,10] with inclusive bounds and floor midpoint.', r'Test the initial middle::low=0,\ high=3,\ mid=1::Four is smaller than seven, so low becomes two.~Test the reduced middle::mid=2::Six is smaller, so low becomes three.~Test the final candidate::mid=3::Ten is larger, making high=2 and low>high.', 'Seven is absent after three midpoint comparisons.', 'The neighboring keys six and ten bracket seven, so the reported absence agrees with the sorted sequence.'),
E('application', 'Find the first two in [1,2,2,2,3] by recording equality and continuing left.', r'Record an interior match::mid=2,\ candidate=2::Equality records an answer but reduces high to one.~Inspect the left interval::mid=0::One is smaller than two, so low becomes one.~Improve the boundary match::mid=1,\ candidate=1::Equality updates the candidate and makes high zero, ending the loop.', 'First occurrence is index 1.', 'Index one contains two and its predecessor contains one, confirming both equality and the left boundary.'),
E('trap', 'Inclusive search on [1,2] seeks two but updates low=mid when the midpoint value is smaller. Does the interval shrink?', r'Compute the midpoint::low=0,\ high=1,\ mid=0::The midpoint value is one.~Apply the flawed update::low\leftarrow mid=0::Neither endpoint changes, so the next iteration tests the same slot.~Repair the rule::low\leftarrow mid+1=1::Removing the disproved midpoint leaves the actual target.', 'The flawed update can loop forever; use mid+1.', 'The corrected candidate interval has one element, which contains two and terminates successfully.'))

P('4-search-sort-merge-sort-full-trace', 'Merge sort', '''Merging requires both child sequences to be sorted before their fronts can determine the next output. The output prefix remains sorted, and every unconsumed child value is no smaller than its own front. This invariant explains correctness and the linear combine cost. It also supports applications such as counting cross inversions: a selected right value smaller than the left front is smaller than every remaining left value. Stability is a separate tie choice: take a left record first on equality to retain original equal-key order through contiguous recursive splits. Exact comparisons exclude appending a remainder after one child empties. Confirm the permutation, sortedness and any additional count derived during merging.''',
E('fundamental', 'Merge sorted children [2,6] and [1,5]; count cross-side comparisons.', r'Select the first two outputs::::Compare two with one and take one; compare two with five and take two.~Exhaust the right side::::Compare six with five and take five.~Append without comparison::::The right side is empty, so append six directly.', 'Result [1,2,5,6] with three cross-side comparisons.', 'The merged list has four elements, and the remainder step never inspects an opposing front.'),
E('application', 'During inversion-counting merge sort, merge left [4,7] and right [1,3]. Count cross inversions.', r'Select right value one::count\leftarrow count+2::One is below both remaining left values four and seven.~Select right value three::count\leftarrow count+2::Three is also below both still-unconsumed left values.~Append the left remainder::::Output four and seven without adding cross inversions.', 'Merged output [1,3,4,7]; four cross inversions.', 'The pairs are (4,1),(7,1),(4,3),(7,3), independently enumerating all four.'),
E('trap', 'Left sorted child [1A,2B], right child [1C,2D] came from contiguous original positions. Merge by taking the right record on equal keys. Is it stable?', r'Resolve the first tie::::Right record 1C precedes left record 1A in the output.~Resolve the second tie::::Right record 2D similarly precedes 2B, producing [1C,1A,2D,2B].~Compare original equal-key order::::A originally preceded C and B preceded D; both orders are reversed.', 'The output is numerically sorted but unstable.', 'Sorted keys alone pass, while the labeled equal-key pairs directly fail the stability requirement.'))

P('4-search-sort-quicksort-lomuto', 'Quicksort', '''Partitioning arranges values around a pivot but does not necessarily sort either resulting side. The returned index or boundary must be interpreted according to the specific scheme, especially when adapting recursive endpoints. Lomuto puts its pivot in a final index, while a Hoare-style boundary need not designate a final pivot location. Equal-key input can expose a poor two-way partition policy even without already sorted distinct keys. To control stack space, recurse only on the smaller side and iterate on the larger; this changes frame growth without eliminating the work of bad partitions. State pivot selection, equality handling and whether expected or worst-case cost is requested before deriving complexity.''',
E('fundamental', 'Lomuto-partition [3,1,2] around last pivot two using ≤ comparisons.', r'Initialize the boundary::::Set i=−1; value three fails the pivot comparison and stays outside the small prefix.~Accept the smaller value::::One succeeds, so increment i to zero and swap to get [1,3,2].~Place the pivot::::Swap pivot two with position i+1=1, yielding [1,2,3].', 'Pivot ends at index 1; array [1,2,3].', 'The one left value is ≤2 and the one right value is >2, verifying the partition invariant.'),
E('application', 'Quicksort repeatedly splits n keys into sizes zero and n−1. It recurses on the smaller side and loops over the larger. Give time and maximum frame growth.', r'Count partition work::n+(n-1)+\cdots+2=\Theta(n^2)::Changing call placement does not reduce the scanned partition sizes.~Follow the recursive side::::The smaller side is empty at each step and returns immediately.~Reuse the large-side frame::::The loop processes the remaining n−1 elements in the current frame rather than pushing a chain.', 'Worst-case time remains Θ(n²), while this extreme-split case uses O(1) simultaneous frames.', 'There are still linearly many increasingly shorter scans, but no retained recursive chain for the large side.'),
E('trap', 'Lomuto with last pivot and ≤ comparisons partitions [5,5,5,5]. Do equal keys guarantee balanced halves?', r'Accept every scanned key::::All three nonpivot fives satisfy ≤5, advancing i to two.~Place the final pivot::::The pivot swaps with itself at index three.~Identify the recursive sizes::3\ \text{and}\ 0::The same pattern recurs on the size-three equal-key prefix.', 'No; all-equal input creates maximally unbalanced splits for this scheme.', 'The recurrence T(n)=T(n−1)+Θ(n) is quadratic; a three-way equal-key partition would change this behavior.'))

P('4-search-sort-heapsort', 'Heapsort', '''Heapsort maintains two regions with different invariants: an active max-heap prefix and an increasing finalized suffix. Root extraction grows the suffix by one maximum, while sifting repairs only the shortened prefix. Keep heap length separate from physical array length; otherwise a large finalized value can be pulled back into the active structure. Bottom-up heap construction costs linear time, followed by logarithmic repairs for a linear number of extractions. Standard swaps need not preserve equal-key record order, so instability must be tested with labels rather than numeric values alone. Verify both regions after each iteration and check that the final suffix becomes the entire nondecreasing array.''',
E('fundamental', 'Finish heapsort on max-heap [6,4,5], showing each active heap and sorted suffix.', r'Extract the first maximum::::Swap six with five, shrink to two and retain prefix [5,4], suffix [6].~Extract the next maximum::::Swap five with four, shrink to one, giving prefix [4], suffix [5,6].~Finish the singleton::::The remaining prefix has one element and needs no repair.', 'Sorted array [4,5,6].', 'Each finalized suffix is increasing and contains values at least as large as all values in the active prefix.'),
E('application', 'For heap [12,9,10,3,6,7], perform two heapsort extractions. Show the arrays and active lengths.', r'Extract twelve::::Move seven to the root, shrink to five and sift with ten, obtaining [10,9,7,3,6,12].~Extract ten::::Move six to the root, shrink to four and sift with nine, obtaining [9,6,7,3,10,12].~Separate regions::::The active heap is [9,6,7,3]; the finalized suffix is [10,12].', 'After two extractions: [9,6,7,3,10,12], active length four.', 'Nine dominates six/seven, six dominates three, and both suffix values exceed every active value.'),
E('trap', 'Heapsort two equal-key labeled records [2A,2B]. Its initial heap is valid. Is the standard root/end-swap version stable?', r'Preserve labels in the heap::::Both keys are two, so no heap-order change is required initially.~Execute the extraction swap::::Root 2A exchanges with final record 2B, giving [2B,2A].~Check relative order::::The numeric order is sorted, but B now precedes originally earlier A.', 'No; standard heapsort can reverse equal-key records.', 'A two-record counterexample suffices to disprove stability even though every heap and sorting key invariant holds.'))

P('4-hashing-chaining', 'Hashing', '''A hash computes a storage address, not a unique identity for a key. Specify the complete key fields, the modulus and any encoding before tracing. Distinct keys can share an address even for a correctly functioning hash, so retrieval must compare the stored keys after locating candidates. Composite keys require all equality-relevant fields to participate in key comparison and preferably in hashing; omitting one field can concentrate otherwise different records. Distribution assumptions support expected complexity but do not establish worst-case constant time. Check computed addresses fall within the table and demonstrate collision handling separately from hashing. A collision is not automatically evidence that either key or its stored value should be replaced.''',
E('fundamental', 'Use h(k)=(2k+1) mod 7 for keys three and ten. Do they collide?', r'Evaluate the first key::h(3)=7\bmod7=0::The computed address lies in slots zero through six.~Evaluate the second key::h(10)=21\bmod7=0::A different key produces the same address.~Interpret the result::::Both need collision resolution; their equality is not implied by equal hashes.', 'The keys collide at slot zero but remain distinct keys.', 'Their difference seven explains the shared residue under this linear modulo-seven hash.'),
E('application', 'A note key is (course,module). Hash h=(31·course+module) mod 11. Compare keys (1,2) and (2,2), then assess hashing module alone.', r'Hash the first composite key::h(1,2)=33\bmod11=0::Both fields contribute to its address.~Hash the second composite key::h(2,2)=64\bmod11=9::The distinct course changes the address even with the same module.~Compare the incomplete scheme::::Hashing only module sends both to two and can heavily cluster equal module numbers across courses.', 'Composite hashes are zero and nine; module-only hashing collides.', 'Full key comparison must still include both fields even when a particular pair has different composite hashes.'),
E('trap', 'With h(k)=k mod 5, bucket three contains key eight. A lookup for thirteen reaches that bucket. Can it return eight\'s value immediately?', r'Locate the shared bucket::8\bmod5=13\bmod5=3::The hash selects candidates rather than proving equality.~Compare actual keys::8\ne13::The stored record does not satisfy the query key.~Complete the lookup::::If the bucket contains no other candidate, thirteen is absent.', 'No; report absence rather than eight\'s value.', 'A direct key equality check refutes the match while retaining the valid hash computation.'))

P('4-hashing-chaining', 'Chaining', '''Chaining attaches a collection of records to each bucket, allowing several keys to occupy one hash address. Search walks only the selected chain but must compare every relevant candidate until it succeeds or exhausts the chain. Exact successful-search cost depends on record position and insertion order, whereas unsuccessful cost depends on the whole chain length. Average chain length n/m does not mean every chain has that length. An empty bucket still has a header even though it contributes zero key comparisons. Growth can improve expected costs without being necessary for basic capacity, because unlike open addressing a chained table may store more records than bucket headers.''',
E('fundamental', 'Insert 1,4,7 in that order at chain tails using h(k)=k mod 3; search for seven.', r'Hash the keys::1\bmod3=4\bmod3=7\bmod3=1::All three records belong to bucket one.~Build the collision chain::::Tail insertion produces 1→4→7 in that bucket.~Compare in order::::The search rejects one and four before matching seven.', 'Seven is found after three key comparisons.', 'The chain retains all distinct keys despite their common bucket, and the supplied insertion rule determines seven\'s position.'),
E('application', 'Buckets are 0:[0], 1:[1,4,7], 2:[]. A query is uniformly chosen among the four stored keys. Find expected key comparisons for successful search.', r'List individual costs::::Queries 0,1,4,7 require respectively 1,1,2,3 key comparisons.~Apply the stated distribution::E[C]=(1+1+2+3)/4::Uniform keys weight each successful position equally.~Compute the mean::E[C]=7/4=1.75::Bucket selection overhead is excluded from the requested key-comparison measure.', 'Expected successful key comparisons are 1.75.', 'The four equally likely explicit traces account for every stored-key query, rather than averaging chain lengths alone.'),
E('trap', 'A chained table has two buckets and keys 0,1,2,3,4 using k mod 2. Is load 2.5 evidence that insertion is impossible?', r'Compute the ratio::\alpha=5/2=2.5::There are more records than bucket headers.~Construct the actual chains::::Bucket zero stores [0,2,4] and bucket one stores [1,3].~Check representation capacity::::Each bucket can hold multiple allocated nodes, so no one-record-per-slot restriction applies.', 'No; all five keys can be stored by chaining.', 'The two displayed chains preserve every key, directly disproving a capacity limit of two.'))

P('4-hashing-probing', 'Open addressing', '''Open addressing must define a probe sequence and a termination rule as well as the initial hash. A probe's offset may depend on both its number and the key, as in double hashing. Full coverage requires an appropriate relation between step and table size; available slots can remain unreachable under a poor step. Search follows the insertion sequence and stops at a never-used empty slot, while tombstones require continuation. Quadratic offsets can revisit slots before visiting all slots, so count actual positions under the stated formula rather than assuming a permutation. Bound attempts to detect sequence repetition or a full cycle, and distinguish occupied, deleted and never-used states.''',
E('fundamental', 'Double hashing has size seven, h1(k)=k mod 7, h2(k)=1+(k mod 6). Insert 10,17,24 into an empty table.', r'Insert the first key::h_1(10)=3,\ h_2(10)=5::Slot three is empty, so ten occupies it.~Resolve the next collision::(3+6)\bmod7=2::Seventeen starts at three but uses step six to find slot two.~Resolve the final collision::(3+1)\bmod7=4::Twenty-four starts at three but uses step one to occupy four.', 'Slots 3,2,4 contain 10,17,24 respectively.', 'Each later lookup retraces its own key-dependent sequence and reaches the displayed slot.'),
E('application', 'Quadratic probing uses (k mod 7+i²) mod 7, i=0,1,... . Insert 0,7,14,21 in order.', r'Place the first two keys::::Zero takes slot zero; seven skips zero and takes slot one.~Place fourteen::i=2\Rightarrow slot=4::Slots zero and one are occupied, so the next quadratic offset four succeeds.~Place twenty-one::i=3\Rightarrow slot=2::After zero,one,four are occupied, offset nine wraps to two.', 'Keys occupy slots 0,1,4,2 in insertion order.', 'The residues of offsets 0,1,4,9 modulo seven are 0,1,4,2, matching every traced probe.'),
E('trap', 'A size-six table starts probing at one with constant step two. Slots 1,3,5 are occupied and 0,2,4 are empty. Can probing find an empty slot?', r'Enumerate reachable positions::1,3,5,1::Modulo-six step two repeats after three positions.~Check the uncovered positions::::All empty slots have even indices and never occur in this sequence.~Identify the cause::\gcd(2,6)=2::The step does not generate every residue class.', 'No; this sequence cycles despite three empty slots.', 'A coprime step would cover six positions; the displayed three-position cycle proves failure under the given step.'))

P('4-hashing-load-factor-resize', 'Load factor', '''Load is a ratio of live records to allocated positions, not a direct measurement of worst collision cost. Chaining interprets it as average chain occupancy, while open addressing requires space for each live record. Tombstones can make probing behave like a nearly occupied table even when the live load is low, so implementations may track used-slot occupancy separately. Resizing thresholds are policy choices and can depend on anticipated insertion or on deletion history. Compare tables with the same live load to understand why distribution and probe history also matter. State whether a count includes live keys, deleted markers or both before applying any expected-performance rule.''',
E('fundamental', 'A chained table stores nine keys in four buckets. Compute load and explain what it says about individual chains.', r'Identify numerator and denominator::::Nine live keys share four bucket headers.~Calculate the average::\alpha=9/4=2.25::This is the mean chain length across all buckets.~Avoid an individual-chain inference::::Some chains can be empty while another is long; the ratio does not require a chain of length 2.25.', 'Load factor is 2.25, an average rather than an exact per-chain length.', 'Summing the integer chain lengths must give nine; dividing that sum by four reproduces the ratio.'),
E('application', 'Size-seven linear-probing table contains 1,8,15 in slots 1,2,3. Search for absent 22 using k mod 7. Count inspected slots and load.', r'Compute live load::\alpha=3/7::Three live records occupy seven positions.~Follow the probe run::::Start at one and reject keys one,eight,fifteen in consecutive slots 1,2,3.~Stop at unused slot four::::The fourth inspected slot is never used, proving twenty-two was not inserted later in this run.', 'Load 3/7; four inspected slots for the unsuccessful lookup.', 'All keys share residue one; their contiguous collision cluster explains the probe count beyond the modest average load.'),
E('trap', 'A size-eight open-addressed table has two live keys, five tombstones and one never-used empty slot. Is live load 0.25 sufficient to promise short probes?', r'Calculate the live ratio::\alpha_{live}=2/8=0.25::Only two records remain live.~Count slots that do not stop search::\alpha_{used}=7/8::Both live entries and tombstones can force continuation.~Assess a long probe route::::A route visiting those seven used positions before the empty one can require eight inspections.', 'No; tombstone history can leave expensive probes despite low live load.', 'The distinction between two live slots and seven nonterminating slots gives an explicit obstruction to the low-load inference.'))

P('4-greedy-divide-greedy-activity-selection', 'Greedy', '''A greedy rule is tied to one objective and feasible set; changes to either can invalidate its proof. Fractional selection allows small exchanges of resource, whereas indivisible items may block them. Coding and scheduling problems use different safe-choice structures even when both repeatedly select a smallest or largest key. For every trace, write the remaining resource or components after the choice, and check that the next choice is feasible. Optimality needs an exchange, cut or inductive argument beyond obtaining a feasible output. A small counterexample can reject a candidate rule by showing a better valid solution, which is especially useful when a familiar rule is transferred to an unfamiliar problem.''',
E('fundamental', 'Fractional knapsack capacity six has items (weight4,value8) and (weight5,value5). Apply value-per-weight greedy.', r'Compare densities::8/4=2>5/5=1::The first item yields more value per capacity unit.~Take the dense item::::Use four units for value eight, leaving capacity two.~Fill fractionally::8+2\cdot1=10::Take two-fifths of the second item for two more value units.', 'Maximum value is 10.', 'The selected weights total six; exchanging any high-density unit for the lower-density item would reduce value.'),
E('application', 'Huffman coding symbol weights are 1,2,3,6. Repeatedly merge the two smallest weights and compute weighted code length.', r'Merge the first pair::1+2=3::The remaining weights are 3,3,6.~Merge the next pair::3+3=6::The two remaining weights are six and six.~Merge the final pair::6+6=12::Total merge weight is 3+6+12=21, the weighted leaf-depth sum.', 'Optimal weighted code length is 21.', 'Weights 1,2 have depth three, weight 3 depth two and weight 6 depth one, giving 3+6+6+6=21.'),
E('trap', 'Coin denominations are 1,3,4 with unlimited copies. Apply largest-coin-first to amount six and compare with a better solution.', r'Follow the local rule::::Choose four, then one, then one to reach six.~Construct another feasible solution::::Two coins of value three also total six.~Compare the objective::2<3::The alternative uses fewer coins, disproving greedy optimality for these denominations.', 'Largest-first returns three coins; the optimum is two coins, 3+3.', 'No single denomination equals six, so two is a matching lower bound on the number of coins.'))

P('4-greedy-divide-divide', 'Divide and conquer', '''Divide-and-conquer analysis begins with what information each child returns and how the parent combines it. Count both child work and combination work, and confirm that child size decreases toward a base case. Independent halves often permit a balanced recurrence, while fast exponentiation creates only one recursive half-sized child and reuses its result. Duplicating that child computation changes the recurrence and can waste substantial work. The Master theorem requires fixed-ratio child sizes; a size-minus-one recurrence must not be forced into its template. Verify recursive outputs on concrete subarrays or values before replacing the execution with a growth bound, and distinguish exact work from asymptotic order.''',
E('fundamental', 'Find the maximum of [7,2,9,4] by recursively splitting into halves and comparing child maxima.', r'Solve the left half::::Compare seven and two to return seven.~Solve the right half::::Compare nine and four to return nine.~Combine child results::::Compare seven and nine to return nine, using three comparisons overall.', 'Maximum nine, found with three comparisons.', 'All four values are no greater than nine, and the count n−1=3 matches the necessary comparison structure.'),
E('application', 'Compute 3^5 using one recursive result p=power(3,floor(n/2)), squaring p and multiplying by three only when n is odd; power(3,0)=1.', r'Reach the base::::The exponent chain is 5→2→1→0, whose result is one.~Build smaller powers::power(3,1)=1^2\cdot3=3,\quad power(3,2)=3^2=9::Each level reuses one returned result.~Finish the odd exponent::power(3,5)=9^2\cdot3=243::The odd adjustment adds the remaining factor.', '3^5=243 with logarithmic recursion depth.', 'Direct multiplication 3·3·3·3·3 also yields 243; no second half-sized recursive computation was needed.'),
E('trap', 'Can the Master theorem for T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n) be applied directly to T(n)=2T(n−1)+1, T(0)=1?', r'Inspect child size::::The child is n−1, not n divided by a fixed constant greater than one.~Expand a few levels::T(1)=3,\ T(2)=7,\ T(3)=15::The invocation growth doubles each level.~Solve the actual pattern::T(n)=2^{n+1}-1::A linear recursion depth with doubling branching gives exponential work.', 'No; use expansion or another recurrence method, yielding Θ(2^n).', 'Substitution gives 2(2^n−1)+1=2^(n+1)−1, verifying the claimed solution.'))

P('4-greedy-divide-exchange-activity-proof', 'Exchange argument', '''An exchange proof identifies exactly what is replaced, what remains unchanged and why the transformed solution remains valid. It may preserve cardinality or improve weight, depending on the objective. Begin with an arbitrary optimum rather than assuming the greedy construction is already optimal. Capacity-preserving fractional exchanges are simpler than indivisible replacements because equal resource amounts can be swapped. In tree problems, adding an edge and removing another from the created cycle preserves connectivity and acyclicity. If a proposed activity replacement forces deletion of a later activity, equal-count preservation fails and the argument collapses. Check feasibility before comparing objective values, and separate the illustrative numeric exchange from the universal inequality needed by the theorem.''',
E('fundamental', 'In fractional knapsack, an alleged optimum uses one weight unit of density two while an unused unit of density five is available. Exchange those units.', r'Preserve the resource::::Remove one used weight unit and add one unused weight unit, keeping total capacity unchanged.~Compute the value change::\Delta v=1\cdot5-1\cdot2=3::The replacement strictly improves value.~Contradict optimality::::A feasible improvement cannot exist in an optimum, so the original selection cannot be optimal.', 'The exchange increases value by three and disproves the alleged optimum.', 'Equal exchanged weights establish feasibility independently of the positive value difference.'),
E('application', 'A spanning tree on exactly vertices A,B,C has edges AB=2 and BC=5. Add available edge AC=1. Identify a cycle exchange and its improvement.', r'Create the unique cycle::::Adding AC makes triangle A–B–C–A.~Remove a heavier cycle edge::::Delete BC=5, leaving AB and AC connecting all three vertices.~Compare total weights::2+1=3<2+5=7::The new spanning tree improves weight by four.', 'Replace BC by AC; the tree weight falls from seven to three.', 'Two edges connect three vertices without a cycle, verifying the transformed object is still a spanning tree.'),
E('trap', 'Shortest-duration activity g=(2,4) replaces a=(0,3) in schedule [a,b], where b=(3,6). Does that exchange preserve feasibility and count?', r'Compare the durations::::g has length two while a and b each have length three.~Test the proposed replacement::::g finishes at four, but b starts at three, so the replaced schedule overlaps.~Account for the forced deletion::::Removing b to restore feasibility leaves one activity instead of the original two.', 'No; shortest duration does not supply a count-preserving exchange.', 'The feasible original [a,b] has count two while any schedule containing g from these three activities has count one.'))

P('4-dynamic-states', 'Dynamic programming', '''A DP state is a precise sentence describing what information has been fixed and what answer is still sought. Choose transitions by partitioning valid possibilities, and arrange computation so every dependency has its correct version. Counts, feasibility, minima and maxima need different base values and different unreachable sentinels. Table order can also change the problem: counting unordered coin combinations differs from counting ordered sequences, and reusing updated capacity states differs from single-use selection. Draw a small table before compressing memory, then trace representative entries back to their dependencies. Verify both the objective value and a concrete witness when possible; a plausible number does not establish that the recurrence models the intended feasible solutions.''',
E('fundamental', 'Count unordered ways to make amount four with unlimited coins one and two. Use coin-outer, increasing-amount tabulation.', r'Initialize the empty amount::::Set ways[0]=1 and other entries zero, representing one empty selection.~Process coin one::::Amounts zero through four become [1,1,1,1,1].~Process coin two::::Updates produce [1,1,2,2,3], with ways[4] adding the two ways at amount two.', 'There are three combinations.', 'The combinations are 1+1+1+1, 1+1+2 and 2+2; reordering the same coins is not counted separately.'),
E('application', 'Compute Levenshtein edit distance from ab to ac with insertion, deletion and substitution each cost one.', r'Initialize empty-prefix states::::The first row and column are 0,1,2 because empty strings require one operation per character.~Use the matching first characters::::The a/a cell is zero; completing its row gives [1,0,1].~Evaluate the final mismatch::::The b/c cell takes one plus the matching-prefix diagonal, giving final row [2,1,1].', 'Edit distance is one.', 'Substituting b by c is a one-operation witness, and unequal strings cannot have distance zero.'),
E('trap', 'A one-array 0/1 knapsack update processes item (weight2,value3) in increasing capacities up to four. Why does it report an invalid value six?', "Update capacity two::dp[2]=3::The item is first included using the old zero state.~Read the already changed dependency::dp[4]=3+dp[2]=6::Increasing order lets the same item's new state feed a second inclusion.~Restore the correct dependency version::::Decreasing capacity order reads the previous-item dp[2]=0 before it is changed.", 'The ascending update solves reuse, not 0/1 selection; correct value is three.', 'Six requires two copies of the only item, directly violating the one-use constraint.'))

P('4-dynamic-optimal-substructure-knapsack', 'Optimal substructure', '''Optimal substructure is a replacement property relative to a sufficient state. An optimal full solution must contain optimal residual answers when replacing them cannot violate constraints outside the state. Shortest paths with ordinary additive weights admit a prefix replacement, while simple-path constraints can make visited vertices relevant. A state that forgets those vertices can invalidate an otherwise convincing replacement. In string alignment, prefix boundaries isolate residual comparisons and support a clean recurrence. Prove feasibility of replacement before claiming its objective improves. Overlap is a separate property about repeated subproblems; having optimal substructure does not alone guarantee that dynamic programming will reduce an exponential number of distinct states.''',
E('fundamental', 'A shortest S-to-T route is S→A→B→T with weights 2,3,1. If another S-to-B route costs four, what follows?', r'Measure the alleged route::2+3+1=6::Its prefix to B costs five.~Replace the prefix::::The four-cost route to B can be followed by the same edge B→T of weight one.~Compare the full cost::4+1=5<6::The replacement contradicts the claimed shortest distance.', 'The original route cannot be shortest if a four-cost S-to-B route exists.', 'The unchanged suffix contributes one in both candidates, isolating the benefit of an optimal prefix.'),
E('application', 'Find the longest common subsequence length of ABC and AC using optimal prefix substructure.', r'Initialize empty prefixes::::A zero-length prefix has common-subsequence length zero with every other prefix.~Process A and B::::Rows after A and B are [0,1,1] and [0,1,1]; unequal final characters use the larger adjacent prefix answer.~Match the final C::::The final row is [0,1,2], taking one plus the previous A-matching prefix length.', 'LCS length is two, realized by AC.', 'AC occurs in order in both strings, and the second string has only two characters, giving a matching upper bound.'),
E('trap', 'Directed edges S→A=1, S→B=1, B→A=1, A→B=100, A→T=1, B→T=1. The longest simple route is S,A,B,T. Can its S-to-A prefix be replaced by the longer S,B,A prefix?', r'Compare prefix weights::::S→A costs one, while S→B→A costs two.~Attempt the replacement::::Following the old suffix A→B→T after S→B→A repeats B.~Check the full constraint::::A simple path cannot revisit a vertex, so the replacement is infeasible despite improving prefix weight.', 'No; an endpoint-only state forgets visited vertices required by the simple-path constraint.', 'The original route weighs 102; feasible alternatives S,A,T; S,B,T; S,B,A,T weigh 2,2,3, so it is indeed longest.'))

P('4-dynamic-memoization-cache-trace', 'Memoization', '''A cache is correct only when its key completely determines the cached result under the current input. State variables such as position, remaining resource or processed-item prefix cannot be omitted just because some calls happen to agree. On a hit, return before expanding dependencies; on a miss, compute and store after all required children are known. Count reachable distinct states for computation work, and count separate requests when measuring cache accesses. Base states may be prefilled, which changes miss counts without changing the mathematical answer. Memoization can save overlap while retaining a deep first recursion chain, and a cyclic dependency needs additional handling rather than indefinite recursive cache misses.''',
E('fundamental', 'Memoize grid-route counts F(i,j)=F(i−1,j)+F(i,j−1), with F(0,j)=F(i,0)=1. Compute F(2,2).', r'Compute the shared interior state::F(1,1)=1+1=2::Both surrounding boundary states are already known.~Compute neighboring states::F(1,2)=2+1=3,\quad F(2,1)=1+2=3::The second request for F(1,1) can reuse the cache.~Combine the requested state::F(2,2)=3+3=6::Only one computation of the shared interior state is required.', 'Six routes; F(1,1) is reused rather than recomputed.', 'Choosing the positions of two horizontal moves among four moves gives binomial(4,2)=6.'),
E('application', 'Memoize minimum coins for amount three with denominations one and three; F(0)=0 is cached and children are tried in denomination order.', r'Descend using coin one::::Requests three,two,one miss in turn before the zero state is hit.~Store the smaller answers::F(1)=1,\quad F(2)=2::Each result is stored after its feasible children return.~Evaluate the direct-three alternative::F(3)=\min(1+2,1+0)=1::The second zero request hits the existing cache.', 'F(3)=1; five total requests, three newly computed states.', 'Request order is 3,2,1,0,0; one coin of value three is a concrete optimal witness.'),
E('trap', 'A 0/1 knapsack cache uses only capacity as its key. Item A=(2,3), B=(2,9). Compare states using first one versus first two items at capacity two.', r'Evaluate the first prefix::F(1,2)=3::Only A is eligible.~Evaluate the longer prefix::F(2,2)=9::B becomes eligible and is the better single item.~Detect the key collision::::Both states would use capacity key two but require different answers.', 'Capacity alone is an invalid key; include the item-prefix state.', 'The distinct correct values three and nine are a direct counterexample to cache reuse across those states.'))

P('4-dynamic-knapsack', 'Knapsack', '''Knapsack variants differ in item reuse and in whether capacity is an upper limit or an exact target. A 0/1 state must limit eligible items; an unbounded variant may keep the current item eligible after inclusion. An at-most-capacity formulation can accept unused space, whereas exact filling needs an unreachable sentinel for positive capacities with no feasible subset. Value-per-weight ranking is not sufficient for indivisible items because a locally attractive choice can block a better combination. Derive the include/exclude alternatives under the chosen variant, then reconstruct selected records and verify weight, value and multiplicity. The O(nW) table bound is pseudopolynomial because numeric W has a logarithmic-length binary encoding.''',
E('fundamental', '0/1 capacity five has A=(weight2,value4), B=(3,5). Compute the optimum by include/exclude decisions.', r'Evaluate the first item::::A fits capacities two through five and yields value four.~Compare choices for B::dp[2,5]=\max(4,5+dp[1,2])=9::Including B leaves capacity two for A.~Reconstruct the subset::::Choose both A and B, each exactly once.', 'Optimal value nine with total weight five.', 'The four subsets have values 0,4,5,9 and weights 0,2,3,5, so the selected feasible maximum is verified.'),
E('application', 'Capacity four and one 0/1 item (weight3,value5): compare at-most-capacity and exactly-filled formulations.', r'Solve the at-most case::::Selecting the item leaves one unused capacity unit and yields five.~Enumerate exact possibilities::::The available subset weights are zero and three, neither equal to four.~Use an unreachable state::::Exact-fill dp[1,4] stays impossible rather than inheriting zero as a feasible filled value.', 'At-most optimum five; exact fill is infeasible.', 'Enumerating the only two subsets independently confirms the different outcomes.'),
E('trap', 'Capacity six, indivisible items A=(4,8), B=(3,5), C=(3,5). Does descending density greedy maximize value?', r'Compare densities::8/4=2>5/3::The density rule chooses A first.~Follow its remaining capacity::::After A, only two units remain and neither B nor C fits, giving value eight.~Construct a better subset::3+3=6,\quad5+5=10::B and C exactly fill the capacity and improve value.', 'No; B,C give optimum ten while greedy gives eight.', 'All other feasible subsets are empty or singletons, so ten is also the exhaustive feasible maximum.'))

P('4-graph-algorithms-traversal-components', 'Graph traversals', '''A traversal's coverage follows the direction and starting policy of its edges. Mark on discovery, so cycles cannot create repeated expansion before a vertex reaches the end of its scan. For a single source, unreachable headers stay unvisited. For full graph coverage, restart at each unvisited header and retain a forest of discovery parents. A directed graph's reachability set is not its undirected component, because following incoming arcs backward changes the problem. Recording discovered vertices, pending work and scanned neighbors makes the invariant visible. Count forest roots separately from vertices and verify that every nonroot has exactly one discovery parent; this gives a useful independent coverage check.''',
E('fundamental', 'Traverse directed cycle A→B→C→A from A using DFS and discovery marking; D is isolated.', r'Discover the first route::::Mark A, then B, then C as their outgoing arcs are followed.~Meet an already discovered vertex::::C sees A visited, so no new recursive expansion begins.~Complete coverage from this source::::Return through C,B,A; D remains unvisited because no permitted route reaches it.', 'Discovery order A,B,C; reachable set {A,B,C}.', 'Each of the three reached vertices is marked once despite the cycle, and D has no incoming route from the set.'),
E('application', 'Undirected graph has vertices 1..6 and edges 1–2,2–3,4–5. Run a full traversal forest and count roots and discovery edges.', r'Explore the first component::::Starting at one discovers two and three, creating two discovery edges.~Restart at unvisited headers::::Four starts a second component and discovers five; six starts a singleton component.~Count the forest::roots=3,\quad edges=6-3=3::Components are {1,2,3},{4,5},{6}.', 'Three roots and three discovery edges.', 'The explicit edge list has exactly the three acyclic component-joining edges reported by the forest.'),
E('trap', 'Directed graph A→B→C is weakly connected. Does a traversal starting at C reach A and B?', r'Inspect permitted outgoing arcs::::C has none, so its expansion finds no new vertex.~Reject reverse traversal::::B→C and A→B cannot be followed backward in this directed model.~Separate connectivity definitions::::Ignoring directions connects all vertices, but that is a different reachability question.', 'Only C is reachable from C.', 'There is no directed path with first step leaving C, so no additional vertex can be reached.'))

P('4-minimum-spanning-cuts', 'Minimum spanning trees', '''A minimum spanning tree optimizes the total weight of a connected acyclic edge set covering all vertices. Negative edges are permitted because tree selection is not a shortest-path finalization problem. Excluding cycles is compulsory even when every cycle edge has negative weight. Uniqueness depends on whether another spanning tree ties the minimum, not simply on whether the graph has repeated weights. Small graphs can be checked by enumerating every V−1-edge subset and rejecting disconnected or cyclic subsets. For parameterized weights, compare candidate tree sums as functions of the parameter. Keep the graph connectedness assumption explicit; disconnected input supports a minimum spanning forest instead of a tree.''',
E('fundamental', 'Undirected triangle has XY=−2, YZ=1, XZ=4. Find its minimum spanning tree.', r'Enumerate valid trees::::Any two triangle edges connect all three vertices without a cycle.~Compare weights::-2+1=-1,\quad-2+4=2,\quad1+4=5::The tree using XY and YZ has the smallest sum.~Verify spanning structure::::Those two edges connect X through Y to Z with exactly V−1 edges.', 'MST edges XY,YZ, total weight −1.', 'All three possible spanning trees were compared, so negative weights introduce no hidden alternative.'),
E('application', 'Triangle weights AB=1, BC=1, AC=x. For what x is the MST unique?', r'Write candidate weights::::Tree AB,BC weighs two; each tree including AC weighs x+1.~Find strict separation::2<x+1\iff x>1::Only then does AB,BC beat both alternatives.~Inspect remaining ranges::::At x=1 all three tie; at x<1 the two AC-containing trees tie for minimum.', 'The MST is unique exactly when x>1.', 'Repeated weights on AB and BC coexist with uniqueness for x>1, disproving the need for all weights to be distinct.'),
E('trap', 'All three edges of a triangle have negative weights −5,−3,−1. Is selecting every negative edge a valid MST?', r'Compare the unconstrained sum::::All three total −9, which is smaller than either two-edge sum.~Check the required structure::::The three edges form a cycle and exceed V−1=2 edges.~Optimize among actual trees::::The valid minimum uses −5 and −3 for total −8.', 'No; the MST contains two edges, total −8.', 'The alternative two-edge totals are −6 and −4; exhaustive tree comparison confirms −8.'))

P('4-shortest-paths-dijkstra', 'Shortest paths', '''Shortest-path reasoning optimizes routes, not the total weight of a network connecting every vertex. State the source, edge direction and whether weights can be negative. DAG order supports relaxation with negative edges because each dependency points forward, while Dijkstra needs nonnegative weights for finalization. A reachable negative cycle destroys a finite distance only for destinations reachable from that cycle; other destinations may retain finite distances. Distances need actual path witnesses, and relaxation inequalities help check that no known edge yields a better route. Infinity represents absence of a route, not a large ordinary integer to which arbitrary arithmetic can safely be applied.''',
E('fundamental', 'Directed edges S→A=2, A→T=3, S→T=8. Find the shortest S-to-T route.', r'List the direct candidate::::The one-edge route costs eight.~Evaluate the indirect route::2+3=5::The route through A is feasible and cheaper.~Exhaust the alternatives::::T has no outgoing edges and no other vertices exist, so these are the only routes.', 'Route S,A,T with distance five.', 'Summing the reported route gives five, and comparing every route verifies optimality.'),
E('application', 'DAG edges S→A=3,S→B=2,A→B=−4,B→T=2,A→T=5. Relax in topological order S,A,B,T.', r'Initialize from S::::Distances become A=3 and B=2.~Process A::d(B)=\min(2,3-4)=-1,\quad d(T)=8::The negative edge improves B before its turn.~Process B::d(T)=\min(8,-1+2)=1::All later dependencies are now finalized by DAG order.', 'Shortest S-to-T path S,A,B,T has cost one.', 'Its weight 3−4+2=1 beats S,B,T of cost four and S,A,T of cost eight.'),
E('trap', 'Edges S→A=0, A→A=−1, S→T=4. Does the reachable negative loop imply no finite shortest distance to T?', r'Locate unbounded routes::::Repeating the loop makes costs to A arbitrarily small.~Check cycle-to-destination reachability::::A has no outgoing route to T or back to S.~Evaluate the unaffected destination::::The only route to T is the direct edge of cost four.', 'T still has finite shortest distance four; A has no finite shortest distance.', 'No path to T can include the loop, which is the missing condition in the blanket negative-cycle claim.'))

P('4-bfs-dfs-bfs', 'BFS', '''Breadth-first search processes a FIFO frontier of discovered but unexpanded vertices. Mark at enqueue time so two parents cannot independently enqueue the same undiscovered vertex. In an unweighted graph, a vertex's level is its minimum number of edges from the source, because every previous level is processed before the next. Parent links recover one shortest-hop route; tie choices depend on neighbor order. BFS levels also support two-coloring an undirected graph by parity, with a same-color edge indicating a bipartite obstruction. Edge weights change the optimization problem: minimizing hops does not minimize arbitrary sums. Verify queue order, discovery marks and distances together instead of presenting only a visited list.''',
E('fundamental', 'Directed lists A:[B], B:[C,D], C:[], D:[]. Run BFS from A in listed order.', r'Expand the source::::Set d(A)=0; discovering B makes queue [B] and d(B)=1.~Expand the first layer::::B discovers C then D, making queue [C,D] with both distances two.~Drain the final layer::::C and D have no outgoing neighbors, so the queue becomes empty.', 'Order A,B,C,D; distances 0,1,2,2.', 'The only routes to C and D use A→B plus one additional edge, agreeing with level two.'),
E('application', 'Use BFS parity colors to test undirected cycle A–B–C–D–A for bipartiteness.', r'Color the root and neighbors::::A receives color zero; B and D at distance one receive color one.~Discover the next level::::C reached through B has distance two and color zero; D sees the already discovered C.~Check every edge::::AB,BC,CD,DA each joins different colors.', 'The graph is bipartite with parts {A,C} and {B,D}.', 'All four edges cross the displayed partition, an independent bipartite witness.'),
E('trap', 'Edges S→T=10,S→A=1,A→T=1. Does ordinary BFS give the minimum-weight route to T?', r'Discover one-hop destinations::::Both T and A enter the first BFS level, so T gets distance one and parent S.~Retain hop-based discovery::::The later two-edge route via A does not improve its already shortest hop count.~Compare weight objectives::1+1=2<10::The extra-hop route is cheaper in total weight.', 'BFS finds minimum hops, but weighted optimum is S,A,T with cost two.', 'The direct route uses one hop and cost ten; the alternative explicitly separates the two objectives.'))

P('4-bfs-dfs-dfs-discovery-finish', 'DFS', '''DFS retains each vertex's suspended neighbor scan while exploring a child. Discovery and completion are separate events, and the active gray vertices form the current recursion path. Directed edge classification depends on that state: a gray target proves a back edge, whereas an already black target may be a cross or forward edge without creating a cycle. In an undirected representation the reverse parent incidence is expected and must be ignored for cycle detection. Deterministic timestamps require a fixed start and neighbor order plus a stated clock convention. Use interval nesting and parent relations to check a trace, and do not treat every previously visited vertex as an active ancestor.''',
E('fundamental', 'Directed chain A→B→C starts DFS at A. Increment time from zero on every discovery and finish.', r'Discover down the chain::d(A)=1,\ d(B)=2,\ d(C)=3::Each caller suspends while its only child is explored.~Finish the leaf::f(C)=4::C has no neighbors, so it returns first.~Finish callers::f(B)=5,\ f(A)=6::Their scans complete after the child returns.', 'Time pairs A=(1,6), B=(2,5), C=(3,4).', 'The nested intervals mirror the ancestor chain A,B,C, and all six events have distinct times.'),
E('application', 'Directed lists A:[B,C], B:[D], C:[D], D:[]. Run DFS from A. Does the later C→D edge prove a cycle?', r'Explore the first branch::::Discovery follows A,B,D; D and then B finish before A resumes.~Explore the second branch::::A discovers C, whose neighbor D is already black and completed.~Classify the later edge::::C→D connects separate explored branches; it does not point to an active ancestor.', 'Discovery A,B,D,C; C→D is not a directed-cycle witness.', 'A,B,C,D is a valid topological order for these edges, independently confirming the graph is acyclic.'),
E('trap', 'Undirected graph has only edge U–V. DFS from U reaches V, whose neighbor U is visited. Is that a cycle?', "Record the parent::::U is V's discovery parent.~Recognize the reverse incidence::::V→U in the list is the same undirected edge just followed outward.~Apply the correct exclusion::::Ignore the parent incidence in the cycle test; no other visited neighbor exists.", 'No; one undirected edge forms a tree, not a cycle.', 'Two vertices and one edge satisfy the connected-tree count V−1=1.'))

P('4-minimum-spanning-kruskal', 'Kruskal', '''Kruskal grows a forest rather than one source-rooted tree. Sort all candidate edges by weight, then ask whether their endpoints have different representatives under the current accepted edges. Acceptance merges two components; rejection prevents a cycle without changing representatives. Negative values and ties do not break the rule, although ties can change which optimum is produced. The connected case ends with V−1 accepted edges, not V−1 processed edges. If processing exhausts the graph first, report the remaining components and a minimum spanning forest. Verify acyclicity using the same component state and sum only accepted weights; discarded edges are still part of processing cost.''',
E('fundamental', 'Triangle edges AB=3, BC=1, AC=2. Trace Kruskal until the tree is complete.', r'Sort candidates::::Process BC,AC,AB by weights one,two,three.~Accept BC::::B and C begin in different singleton components, so merge them.~Accept AC::::A is outside the B,C component; adding AC joins all three vertices and completes two edges.', 'MST edges BC,AC, weight three.', 'The remaining AB would create a cycle; comparing alternative tree sums four and five confirms the optimum.'),
E('application', 'Vertices A,B,C,D,E with edges AB=1,BC=2,AC=3,DE=4. Run Kruskal and interpret the result.', r'Merge the first component::::Accept AB and BC; reject AC because its endpoints already share a component.~Merge the other pair::::Accept DE, joining D and E separately from A,B,C.~Check completion::accepted=3<5-1::No candidate connects the two remaining components.', 'Minimum spanning forest {AB,BC,DE}, weight seven; no spanning tree exists.', 'The forest has V−c=5−2=3 edges, matching the two explicit components.'),
E('trap', 'Triangle edges AB=−5,BC=−3,AC=−1. Does Kruskal accept all three because each lowers the numeric sum?', r'Accept the first negative edges::::AB and BC join three vertices without a cycle.~Check the final representatives::::A and C are now in the same component.~Reject the final edge::::AC is excluded by the tree constraint despite its negative weight.', 'Only AB and BC are accepted, total −8.', 'Two accepted edges connect three vertices; accepting AC would violate acyclicity.'))

P('4-minimum-spanning-prim', 'Prim', '''Prim maintains the cheapest known attachment edge for every outside vertex. A key records one crossing edge's weight, not the whole path from the start. Adding a vertex changes the cut, so its outgoing edges can improve outside keys even when the new path to that vertex would be longer. Internal edges are excluded because they add no new vertex. The cut-safety argument permits negative weights, unlike Dijkstra's proof. An infinity key means no crossing connection is currently known; if all remaining keys are infinite in a disconnected graph, one source tree cannot finish. Record each key's parent edge and verify the final V−1-edge connected structure separately from any distance calculation.''',
E('fundamental', 'Start Prim at A with AB=3,AC=1,BC=2,BD=4,CD=5.', "Choose the first crossing edge::::AC=1 beats AB=3, so C joins the tree.~Improve an attachment::::From C, B improves to key two via BC; D gets key five via CD. Choose B.~Update and attach D::::From B, BD=4 improves D's key from five to four; choose it.", 'Tree AC,CB,BD, total weight seven.', 'All four vertices are connected by three edges, each chosen across the current tree/outside cut.'),
E('application', 'Graph AB=5,AC=2,CB=4. Prim starts at A. Compare B\'s attachment key and its path length in the resulting tree.', "Attach C first::::AC=2 is the cheapest initial crossing edge.~Update the outside key::::CB=4 improves B's attachment key from five to four.~Separate key from distance::path(A,C,B)=2+4=6::The key remains four even though its tree route from A costs six.", 'Prim uses AC,CB: B key four, tree-path length six; direct shortest distance is five.', 'The tree total six beats the alternative AB,AC total seven, while the direct AB edge independently verifies the shorter route objective.'),
E('trap', 'Vertices A,B,C,D have only AB=1 and CD=2. Run Prim from A without restarting. Can it produce a spanning tree?', r'Grow the reachable piece::::A attaches B using the only crossing edge.~Inspect remaining keys::::C and D still have infinity keys because no edge crosses from {A,B}.~Respect disconnectedness::::No next finite attachment exists, so one tree cannot cover every vertex.', 'No spanning tree exists; restarting at C yields a forest with total weight three.', 'The two components have no connecting edge, so connectedness is impossible regardless of key policy.'))

P('4-shortest-paths-dijkstra', 'Dijkstra', '''Dijkstra's correctness comes from finalizing the smallest tentative distance when no negative edge can lead to a cheaper later detour. Tentative values are upper bounds witnessed by known routes, and relaxation can improve them several times before finalization. A lazy heap may therefore contain older entries for the same vertex. Compare a popped entry against the current stored distance or settled state before scanning its edges again. Nonnegative includes zero, so ties need not break correctness, though parent choices may differ. Show the finalized order and actual relaxations, then verify every returned distance using a route and the absence of any improving edge inequality under the assumptions.''',
E('fundamental', 'Directed edges S→A=5,S→B=2,B→A=1,A→C=2,B→C=6. Run Dijkstra from S.', r'Expand the source::::Tentative distances are A=5 and B=2, so B is next.~Relax from B::d(A)=3,\quad d(C)=8::The route through B improves A and discovers C.~Finish remaining vertices::::Finalize A=3, improve C to five, then finalize C.', 'Order S,B,A,C; distances A=3,B=2,C=5.', 'Witness routes S,B,A and S,B,A,C have weights three and five; the direct alternatives are no smaller.'),
E('application', 'Lazy-heap Dijkstra has S→A=10,S→B=1,B→A=2,A→T=1. Which A entries are inserted and what happens to the stale one?', r'Insert initial candidates::::Expanding S inserts A at ten and B at one.~Improve and finalize A::::Expanding B inserts A at three; popping three finalizes A and gives T=4.~Discard the old entry::::When A=10 later pops, its value differs from stored distance three, so skip its adjacency scan.', 'A entries are ten and three; the ten entry is stale and skipped. T distance is four.', 'The route S,B,A,T weighs 1+2+1=4, and stale-entry removal does not remove its valid predecessor chain.'),
E('trap', 'Finalized vertices are never reopened. Edges S→A=2,S→B=5,B→A=−4,A→T=2. Show why Dijkstra\'s assumptions fail.', r'Follow the premature order::::After S, finalize A=2 and then T=4 before B=5.~Reveal the negative detour::5-4=1::Expanding B discovers a cheaper route to already finalized A.~Propagate the true route::1+2=3::Reopening A would be required to improve T, which this version forbids.', 'It can return A=2,T=4 incorrectly; correct values are A=1,T=3.', 'The route S,B,A,T costs 5−4+2=3 and contains no cycle, so a negative edge alone suffices to invalidate finalization.'))

P('4-shortest-paths-bellman-ford-passes', 'Bellman Ford', '''Bellman–Ford replaces greedy finalization with repeated checks of every directed edge. Its pass guarantee is a lower bound on how far route information has propagated; a favorable in-place order may propagate farther within the same pass. A full unchanged pass is a fixed point and allows early termination. After V−1 passes, an extra reachable relaxation signals a reachable negative cycle, whose effects must then be propagated to destinations it can reach. A negative cycle elsewhere in the graph is irrelevant to source reachability. Guard infinity before arithmetic, retain predecessor updates when reconstructing finite routes, and distinguish detecting a cycle from deciding exactly which distances are unbounded below.''',
E('fundamental', 'Process edges S→A=2,S→B=4,A→B=−3 in that order each pass. Initialize only S to zero and run until unchanged.', r'Relax source edges::::The first two edges set A=2 and B=4.~Use the improved source state::d(B)=\min(4,2-3)=-1::The final edge improves B during the same first pass.~Check the next pass::::All three candidate inequalities already hold, so no value changes.', 'Distances S=0,A=2,B=−1; stop after the second, unchanged pass.', 'The route S,A,B costs −1, and the direct S,B route costs four.'),
E('application', 'Edges S→A=1,A→B=−2,B→A=0,B→T=3. Which source distances become unbounded below?', r'Find the cycle weight::-2+0=-2::A→B→A decreases route cost each repetition.~Establish source access::::S reaches A, so this negative cycle is reachable.~Propagate destination effects::::The cycle reaches A,B and T via B→T, but has no route back to S.', 'A,B,T have no finite shortest distance; S remains zero.', 'Repeating the cycle k times lowers each affected route by 2k, while no route returning to S exists.'),
E('trap', 'Edges S→T=2,X→Y=−3,Y→X=1. Source is S. Does the negative X,Y cycle make the extra Bellman–Ford pass improve anything?', r'Initialize reachability::::S=0 and T becomes two; X and Y remain infinity.~Skip unreachable relaxations::::Neither X→Y nor Y→X has a finite source distance, so they cannot create a source route.~Check the additional pass::::Every reachable distance is unchanged even though the separate cycle has weight −2.', 'No extra improvement; T remains two and X,Y remain unreachable.', 'There is no edge from {S,T} to {X,Y}, independently confirming the cycle is outside source reachability.'))

P('4-bfs-dfs-dfs', 'Topological order', '''A topological order is a sequence constrained by every directed edge, not by label size or source distance. Kahn's method maintains current indegrees after removing earlier vertices; a vertex becomes eligible only when all remaining predecessors are gone. Multiple eligible vertices mean multiple choices and potentially multiple valid orders. Counting orders can reduce to interleaving independent chains, but shared dependencies require closer analysis. A cycle forces mutually incompatible earlier-than relations and leaves vertices unremoved. Verify an alleged order by mapping labels to positions and checking every edge. Reverse DFS finish order is another method only after acyclicity is established; the existence of a finish sequence alone is insufficient.''',
E('fundamental', 'Edges P→Q,P→R,Q→S,R→S, plus isolated T. Use FIFO Kahn with initial queue [P,T] and P\'s neighbors Q then R.', r'Remove the first root::::P reduces Q and R to zero indegree; queue becomes [T,Q,R].~Process the independent root and prerequisites::::Remove T, then Q, then R; only after R does S reach zero indegree.~Finish the dependent::::Append and remove S after both of its predecessors.', 'Order P,T,Q,R,S.', 'All four dependency edges point forward, and isolated T may appear without violating any edge.'),
E('application', 'A four-vertex DAG has only A→C and B→D. How many topological orders exist?', r'Identify independent chains::::Each valid order preserves A before C and B before D.~Choose chain positions::\binom{4}{2}=6::Select the two positions occupied by A,C; the remaining positions contain B,D in that order.~Enumerate to verify::::Orders are ACBD,ABCD,ABDC,BDAC,BADC,BACD.', 'Six topological orders.', 'Every listed order preserves both constraints, and each distinct choice of A,C positions appears once.'),
E('trap', 'Directed edges A→B,B→C,C→A. Can any numerical permutation be a topological order?', r'Translate the constraints::pos(A)<pos(B)<pos(C)::The first two edges require these strict inequalities.~Add the final edge::pos(C)<pos(A)::The cycle demands C precede A as well.~Derive the contradiction::::Combining them requires pos(A)<pos(A), which is impossible.', 'No topological order exists.', 'Kahn starts with all indegrees one and an empty eligible queue, providing an independent cycle obstruction.'))

P('4-minimum-spanning-cut-exchange-proof', 'Cut property', '''The cut property concerns a specified partition and only its crossing edges. A lightest crossing edge is safe for some MST, and strict uniqueness strengthens that to membership in every MST. The word some matters when ties exist, including parallel edges with equal weights. An edge that is globally cheapest but internal to one side does not participate in that cut's comparison. When using the property incrementally, choose a cut compatible with already selected safe edges so the exchange preserves their inclusion. Verify the endpoints of every candidate and check the exact quantifier requested. A spanning tree must cross every nontrivial cut, which supplies the structural reason an exchange is available.''',
E('fundamental', 'Triangle edges XY=1,YZ=4,XZ=3. For cut {Y}|{X,Z}, identify the uniquely lightest crossing edge.', r'Classify edges by endpoint sides::::YX and YZ cross the cut; XZ lies entirely on the other side.~Compare crossing weights::1<4::XY is uniquely lightest among the relevant candidates.~Apply the strict theorem::::Every MST must include XY; omitting it would allow a strictly improving cut exchange.', 'XY is mandatory in every MST.', 'The tree XY,XZ weighs four, less than the two alternative tree weights five and seven.'),
E('application', 'Two vertices A,B have parallel edges e1 and e2, both weight two. Apply the cut property to {A}|{B}. Is either edge mandatory?', r'Identify tied minima::::Both edges cross the only nontrivial cut and both are lightest.~Construct the optimal trees::::A tree consists of either e1 alone or e2 alone, each with weight two.~Use the correct quantifier::::Each edge belongs to some MST, but neither belongs to every MST.', 'Both are safe choices; neither is mandatory.', 'The two explicit optimum trees each omit the other edge, disproving compulsory membership.'),
E('trap', 'Cut {A,B}|{C,D} has edges AB=1,CD=1,AC=5,BD=6. Can the weight-one edges defeat AC as the lightest crossing choice?', r'Inspect cheap endpoints::::AB stays inside the first side and CD inside the second.~List actual crossing edges::::Only AC and BD connect opposite sides, with weights five and six.~Apply the comparison::5<6::AC is uniquely lightest for this cut regardless of cheaper internal edges.', 'No; AC is the uniquely lightest crossing edge.', 'AB,CD,AC form a spanning tree of weight seven; the internal cheap edges complement rather than replace the required crossing edge.'))

P('4-minimum-spanning-disjoint-set-compression', 'Disjoint sets', '''A union–find state represents an equivalence partition, so parent-array shape can change without altering which elements are connected. Find must reach a root before union chooses which tree to attach. Attaching an arbitrary nonroot instead can undermine rank reasoning and correctness under some updates. A successful union reduces the component count by one; a redundant union does not. Path compression and rank work together, but rank remains a historical balancing bound rather than the actual height after compression. Record representatives before each union or graph-edge decision, then verify final equivalence classes independently of the particular parent links selected by tie policy.''',
E('fundamental', 'Elements 1,2,3 start separate. Union(1,2) attaches root two to one; union(2,3) attaches root three to the first set. Then union(1,3).', r'Merge the first pair::::Parents become 1→1,2→1,3→3 and component count drops to two.~Find before the second merge::::find(2)=1 and find(3)=3, so attach three to one and reduce count to one.~Reject a redundant merge::::find(1)=find(3)=1, so the final union changes neither parents nor count.', 'Parents all lead to one; exactly two unions merge components.', 'Starting with three components and subtracting two successful merges gives the final one component.'),
E('application', 'Parent links A→A,B→A,C→C,D→C represent two graph components. Consider edge B–D, union its roots by attaching C to A, then find(D) with compression.', r'Compare edge endpoints::::find(B)=A and find(D)=C, so the edge joins distinct components rather than creating a cycle.~Merge roots::::Set C.parent=A; D still temporarily points through C.~Compress the final find::::D follows D→C→A, then its parent becomes A.', 'The edge is accepted; final parents A→A,B→A,C→A,D→A.', 'All four vertices have the same representative after exactly one component-reducing union.'),
E('trap', 'Union by rank builds A with children B and C, C with child D; A rank is two. Full compression makes B,C,D direct children of A. Must A\'s rank drop to one?', r'Measure the compressed shape::::After compression the actual parent-tree height is one.~Recall the rank invariant::::Rank records a balancing history and remains an upper bound; compression does not decrease it.~Preserve future union rules::::Keep rank two even though the current height is smaller.', 'No; rank stays two while actual height becomes one.', 'A upper bound of two remains valid for height one, so retaining it is consistent with the rank method.'))

P('4-shortest-paths-floyd', 'Floyd Warshall', '''Floyd–Warshall's state restricts which vertices may occur inside a route. The outer intermediate loop extends that allowed set one vertex at a time, explaining why its placement differs from an arbitrary relaxation nest. Initialize zero-length self routes, supplied direct edges and infinity for absent routes, taking care with negative self-loops. Each improvement can update a next-hop record for reconstruction; the numeric minimum alone does not identify a unique path. Negative diagonal entries expose cycles, but only pairs able to enter and leave an affected cycle have unbounded route costs. Verify completed distances with concrete routes or triangle inequalities, and use a counterexample rather than assuming all loop permutations compute the same closure.''',
E('fundamental', 'Edges A→B=4,B→C=−1,A→C=9, no other edges. Show the A,C update when B becomes allowed.', r'Read previous bounds::::A→C currently costs nine; A→B and B→C cost four and minus one.~Form the new candidate::4+(-1)=3::The route through B is available under the extended intermediate set.~Select the minimum::D_{AC}=\min(9,3)=3::The shorter indirect route replaces the direct bound.', 'A-to-C distance becomes three.', 'Path A,B,C weighs three and contains the only possible intermediate, so no further route improves it.'),
E('application', 'Edges A→B=2,B→C=3,C→D=1,A→D=10. Run Floyd–Warshall with intermediate order A,B,C,D and give finite final distances.', r'Allow B::::A→C improves to five through A,B,C; other new reverse routes remain absent.~Allow C::::A→D improves to six and B→D to four using C→D.~Read the completed forward matrix::::Rows are [0,2,5,6], [∞,0,3,4], [∞,∞,0,1], [∞,∞,∞,0].', 'Finite forward distances are AB=2,AC=5,AD=6,BC=3,BD=4,CD=1.', 'Each is the sum along its chain segment; the direct AD edge costs ten and cannot improve the six-cost chain.'),
E('trap', 'Vertex order A,B,C,D; edges A→D=1,D→C=1,C→B=1. A single i,j,k loop nest uses k innermost. Why can it miss A→B?', r'Process pair A,B too early::::At that point A→C and D→B are both infinity, so no intermediate gives a finite candidate.~Discover needed pieces later::::Pair A,C later becomes two through D; row D later obtains D→B=2 through C.~Notice no revisit::::The already processed pair A,B stays infinity even though the three-edge route costs three.', 'The wrong loop order can miss AB=3; keep k outermost.', 'With k outermost, allowing C produces DB=2, then allowing D produces AB=1+2=3, confirming the counterexample.'))
